<?php
/**
 * Course-specific curriculum outlines.
 *
 * Each course has six unique lessons. Extra workshop / shipping lessons
 * are appended in curriculum.php based on difficulty.
 *
 * @return array<string, array<int, array{title:string,excerpt:string,detail:string,topics:array<int,string>,practice:string}>>
 */
function lac_curriculum_catalog() {
	return array(
		'generative-ai-foundations' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What generative AI is (and is not)', 'Separate useful generation from search, rules, and hype.', 'Generative models predict the next piece of content from a prompt. They do not look up a private source of truth unless you connect one. This lesson names what the model is good at — drafting, transforming, exploring — and where it fails: citations, live facts, and anything you cannot verify. You will finish able to tell a teammate when generation helps and when a spreadsheet or a search box is the better tool.', array( 'Generation versus retrieval versus rules', 'Typical failure modes you should expect', 'A one-sentence definition you can reuse' ), 'Write two columns: three tasks you would give a generative model this week, and three you would not.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Prompt patterns that actually work', 'Use task, audience, format, and constraints instead of a vague wish.', 'A strong prompt is a job description. You will practice a four-part pattern: the job, who it is for, the output shape, and the limits (length, tone, what to avoid). You will also see why “act as an expert” is weaker than showing one example. Save a template you can paste at the start of any session.', array( 'Task, audience, format, constraints', 'One example beats a long persona', 'Saving a reusable starter prompt' ), 'Rewrite one vague prompt from your week using the four-part pattern and compare the two outputs.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Evaluating model outputs without guessing', 'Score drafts on usefulness, accuracy, and missing pieces.', 'Output that sounds confident is not the same as output you can use. You will score a draft on three checks: did it do the job, did it invent details, and what is missing. Then you revise once with a tighter prompt instead of chatting in circles. That habit is the difference between a toy and a workflow.', array( 'A three-point output rubric', 'Spotting invented facts and vague claims', 'One revision pass, then stop' ), 'Take one AI draft and mark it pass/fail on usefulness, accuracy, and completeness. Revise once.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Safety, attribution, and what you can publish', 'Know what must be checked before a draft leaves your desk.', 'Generative tools can leak private text you pasted, copy a style too closely, or state guesses as facts. This lesson covers a short pre-publish checklist: secrets, citations, people mentioned, and whether a human still owns the final wording. You will also note when to label AI-assisted work for your team.', array( 'What never belongs in a prompt', 'Citing and labeling AI-assisted drafts', 'A pre-publish safety checklist' ), 'Run the checklist on one draft you would actually send. List anything you had to change by hand.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A beginner workflow you can repeat', 'Move from idea to usable draft in a fixed sequence.', 'You now have the pieces. This lesson stitches them: collect inputs, run the starter prompt, score the draft, revise once, then ship or park it. You will time-box each step so the workflow fits a real afternoon. The artifact is the sequence written on one page.', array( 'Inputs worth collecting first', 'First pass versus revision', 'When the draft is good enough to use' ), 'Run the full sequence on a real email, summary, or outline and save the one-page workflow.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Where to go next in this catalog', 'Pick the next course that matches the job you actually have.', 'Foundations are enough to start, not enough to specialize. You will map your next step: prompting depth, writing workflows, or building apps. Choose one course and one practice project for the next two weeks so the skill does not sit idle.', array( 'Matching the next course to a real job', 'A two-week practice project', 'What to keep from this course' ), 'Name the next course and the project you will finish in two weeks. Put both on your calendar.' ),
		),
		'build-ai-apps-with-apis' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Choosing the right model endpoint', 'Match chat, embeddings, and vision APIs to the product job.', 'APIs are not interchangeable. Chat endpoints draft and decide, embeddings retrieve, vision reads images, and speech handles audio. This lesson maps each to a product job and names the constraints that should drive the choice: latency, cost, context length, and structured output. You will leave with a decision table, not a brand preference.', array( 'Chat versus embeddings versus multimodal', 'Latency, cost, and context as design inputs', 'A decision table for one feature' ), 'Pick one feature you want to ship and write which endpoint it needs and why the others are a poor fit.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Auth, rate limits, and a safe first request', 'Call the API without leaking keys or melting a quota.', 'The first production bug is often a key in a screenshot or a loop that retries forever. You will store secrets correctly, set timeouts, log request ids, and handle 429s with backoff. Then you will send one successful request and print the token usage so cost is visible from day one.', array( 'Where API keys belong', 'Timeouts, retries, and rate limits', 'Reading usage from the first response' ), 'Make one authenticated request, capture the usage fields, and write the retry rule you would use in production.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Structured outputs and tool use', 'Get JSON you can trust instead of a paragraph you have to parse.', 'Products break when the model returns a poem instead of a schema. You will request structured output, validate it, and fail closed when the shape is wrong. Then you will sketch one tool call: the model chooses a function, your code runs it, and the result comes back. That loop is how apps do work instead of only chatting.', array( 'Schemas and validation', 'Failing closed on bad JSON', 'A single tool-call round trip' ), 'Define a JSON schema for one feature and reject an invalid model response in code or pseudocode.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Caching, cost, and latency in the UX', 'Hide wait time and avoid paying twice for the same prompt.', 'Users feel latency before they feel intelligence. You will stream tokens when it helps, show a status for slow tools, and cache repeated prompts. You will also estimate cost per 1,000 requests so a “clever” extra call is a product decision, not an accident.', array( 'Streaming versus waiting for the full reply', 'What is safe to cache', 'A back-of-the-envelope cost estimate' ), 'Storyboard one AI screen: what the user sees at 0s, 2s, and 8s, and which calls you would cache.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Shipping a chat feature end-to-end', 'Assemble messages, memory, and errors into a usable thread.', 'A chat feature is a list of messages, a system prompt, and honest errors. You will design the thread, cap history, and decide what is stored. Then you will handle empty replies, moderation blocks, and timeouts with copy a person can understand. The result is a vertical slice you could demo.', array( 'Message lists and history caps', 'What to persist between sessions', 'Error copy users can act on' ), 'Write the message flow for one chat use case including the empty and timeout states.' ),
			lac_cl( 'From demo to a maintainable service', 'Add logging, eval hooks, and an owner before you call it shipped.', 'Demos rot. This lesson adds the production minimum: structured logs, a tiny eval set, and a named owner for the prompt. You will also list the first three alerts you would want (error rate, cost spike, latency). That is how an API feature survives the second month.', array( 'Logs you can debug from', 'A small eval set on the critical path', 'Alerts for errors, cost, and latency' ), 'Write a one-page runbook for your chat slice: logs, evals, and who gets paged.' ),
		),
		'ai-agents-automation' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Agent architectures that scale', 'Pick a loop you can explain: plan, act, observe, stop.', 'An agent is a loop with a stop condition, not a magic employee. You will compare single-shot prompts, tool-using assistants, and multi-step planners. Then you will choose one architecture for a job you actually have and write the stop rules so it cannot run forever. Clarity here prevents most “agent” failures later.', array( 'Single shot versus tool-using versus planners', 'Stop conditions and budgets', 'Choosing one architecture for a real job' ), 'Draw the loop for one automation you want. Label plan, act, observe, and stop.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Tool calling and guardrails', 'Let the model choose tools without giving it the keys to production.', 'Tools are where agents create value and where they cause damage. You will define a small tool list with tight inputs, confirm high-risk actions, and deny anything that sends money or deletes data without a human. Guardrails belong in code, not in a polite system prompt.', array( 'A minimal tool list with strict schemas', 'Human confirmation for risky actions', 'Denying dangerous tools in code' ), 'List five tools for your agent. Mark which require confirmation and which are blocked.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Memory and long-running tasks', 'Store only what the next step needs, then pick the work back up.', 'Agents that remember everything get slower and more confused. You will separate scratch notes, durable facts, and transcripts. For long jobs you will checkpoint progress so a crash does not restart from zero. The design question is always: what must be true when this process wakes up tomorrow?', array( 'Scratch versus durable memory', 'Checkpoints for long jobs', 'What to reload after a crash' ), 'Write the memory fields for a multi-hour task and the checkpoint you would save every 10 minutes.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Recovering from tool and model failures', 'Turn errors into retries, fallbacks, or a clean handoff to a human.', 'Tools time out. Models refuse. This lesson builds a recovery table: retry, skip, ask a human, or abort. You will also write the user-visible message for each path. An agent that fails loudly is safer than one that invents a success.', array( 'Retry versus skip versus abort', 'Handoff copy for a human', 'Never inventing a successful tool result' ), 'Fill a recovery table for three failures you expect in your automation.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Evaluating agent reliability', 'Measure task success, not how clever the trace looks.', 'Traces are interesting; completion rate is the product. You will define success for a task, build a small suite of scenarios, and track steps-to-done and human takeovers. Then you will change one prompt or tool and rerun the suite so you know if you improved anything.', array( 'A definition of task success', 'A small scenario suite', 'Steps-to-done and takeover rate' ), 'Write five scenarios for your agent and the pass/fail rule for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Putting an agent into a real workflow', 'Place the loop next to tickets, inboxes, or cron — with an owner.', 'Agents that live only in a notebook never pay off. You will pick a trigger (schedule, webhook, button), the output destination, and the person who reviews exceptions. Then you will write the first-week watch list. Shipping is the architecture plus the operating habit.', array( 'Triggers that start the loop', 'Where outputs land', 'An owner for exceptions' ), 'Write the trigger, destination, and owner for your first production agent run.' ),
		),
		'ai-basics-your-first-prompt' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What a prompt is really asking for', 'Treat the prompt as a job description, not a magic phrase.', 'Your first prompt works when the model can tell what to do, for whom, and in what shape. This lesson strips away myth and shows a three-line starter: task, audience, format. You will compare a vague wish with a clear request so you can see the difference in the output, not just in theory.', array( 'Task, audience, and format', 'Why vague prompts wander', 'A three-line starter you can reuse' ), 'Rewrite one request from your week as three lines: task, audience, format.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Give the model the right ingredients', 'Decide what context to paste and what to leave out.', 'More text is not always more help. You will practice attaching only the facts the job needs, naming the source of those facts, and cutting anything private. A short, relevant paste beats a whole document dump that buries the instruction.', array( 'Relevant context versus noise', 'What never to paste', 'Labeling where facts came from' ), 'Take one document and paste only the five lines the model actually needs for the task.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Specify the output so you can use it', 'Ask for bullets, a table, or a length you can drop into work.', 'If you do not name the shape, you get an essay. You will request lists, tables, subject lines, and word limits, then check that the result fits the place it will live — email, slide, or ticket. Format is part of the job, not decoration.', array( 'Lists, tables, and length limits', 'Matching output to the destination', 'Asking for a revision of shape only' ), 'Ask for the same answer as bullets and as a 50-word summary. Keep the one you would actually send.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Iterate once with a tighter follow-up', 'Change one thing at a time instead of starting over.', 'Beginners restart the chat when the draft is 70% right. You will practice a single follow-up: keep the good parts, fix one defect, and stop. That is faster than a new prompt and teaches you what the model already understood.', array( 'Name what to keep', 'Fix one defect per follow-up', 'Knowing when to stop' ), 'Take a mediocre draft and send one follow-up that changes only tone or only length.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A first-prompt checklist', 'Run a 30-second check before you hit enter.', 'The checklist is the skill. You will run through task, audience, format, context, and privacy every time. After a week it becomes automatic. This lesson prints the list on one page so you can keep it next to the chat.', array( 'The five-point pre-send check', 'Privacy on every prompt', 'Keeping the list visible' ), 'Print or pin the checklist and use it on three real prompts today.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Your first complete example', 'Finish one useful artifact from a blank box.', 'Theory ends here. You will pick a real task, run the checklist, generate, revise once, and save the prompt that worked. That saved prompt is the start of your personal library for the rest of the catalog.', array( 'Picking a task with a finish line', 'Saving the prompt that worked', 'Reusing it tomorrow' ), 'Complete one artifact (email, summary, or plan) and save the prompt beside it.' ),
		),
		'chatgpt-for-everyday-tasks' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Where ChatGPT saves time in a normal day', 'Pick tasks that are drafting, transforming, or organizing — not deciding for you.', 'Everyday use fails when people ask the model to make high-stakes choices. This lesson maps a typical day: inbox, notes, plans, and explanations. You will mark which tasks are good first uses and which still need you. The goal is a short personal list, not a hundred tricks.', array( 'Drafting versus deciding', 'A personal list of high-value tasks', 'What to keep human' ), 'Circle five recurring tasks this week that are drafting or organizing, not final decisions.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Inbox and calendar: replies, summaries, and agendas', 'Turn messy threads into replies and meeting plans you can send.', 'Threads are long; your time is not. You will summarize a thread into decisions and open questions, draft a reply in your voice, and build a meeting agenda from scattered notes. Always paste only what the other people already know you have.', array( 'Thread summaries with open questions', 'Replies in your voice', 'Agendas from messy notes' ), 'Summarize one real thread into decisions, owners, and open questions. Draft the reply separately.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Notes, docs, and “explain this to me”', 'Compress reading and turn jargon into something you can use.', 'You will practice explain-like-I-work-here: same facts, less fog. Then you will turn a long note into action items. The model is a compressor and translator, not a source of new research unless you provide the source text.', array( 'Explanations at the right altitude', 'Action items from notes', 'Staying faithful to the source' ), 'Paste a page of notes and ask for action items with owners. Check every item against the original.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Lists, tables, and plans you can execute', 'Ask for structure that drops into a doc or tracker.', 'Everyday work lives in lists. You will request tables, checklists, and sequenced plans with time estimates you then edit. If the plan is fantasy, you cut it. The model proposes; you own the calendar.', array( 'Checklists and tables', 'Plans with editable timeboxes', 'Cutting steps you will not do' ), 'Turn one goal into a table of steps, owners, and a realistic timebox. Delete two steps.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Voice, tone, and staying yourself', 'Keep your wording so AI help does not sound like a press release.', 'Default ChatGPT tone is generic. You will feed two samples of your writing, ask it to match, and ban phrases you never use. Then you will read the draft out loud. If it does not sound like you, it is not done.', array( 'Style samples', 'Banned phrases', 'Reading drafts out loud' ), 'Give two short samples of your writing and regenerate one reply until it sounds like you.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A daily 15-minute ChatGPT habit', 'Use the same three prompts every weekday so the skill sticks.', 'Habits beat trick lists. You will pick three standing prompts — summarize, draft, plan — and run them on real work for five days. Track minutes saved. That log tells you whether the tool is earning a place in your day.', array( 'Three standing prompts', 'A five-day practice log', 'Minutes saved versus minutes spent' ), 'Run the three prompts daily for a week and note one win and one miss each day.' ),
		),
		'write-better-emails-with-ai' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What a good email is trying to do', 'Name the ask, the reader, and the next action before you draft.', 'Most emails fail because the writer starts typing before they know the job. You will write the purpose in one line, name the reader’s knowledge, and define the single next action. Only then do you open the model. This is the same discipline as a good prompt, applied to email.', array( 'Purpose in one line', 'What the reader already knows', 'One next action' ), 'Before drafting, write purpose, reader, and next action for three emails you need to send.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Subject lines and openings that get to the point', 'Lead with the request, not a weather report.', 'You will generate subject line options, pick the one that contains the ask, and write an opening sentence that could stand alone. AI is useful for options; you are useful for choosing the honest one. No “just circling back” without a reason.', array( 'Subject lines that contain the ask', 'Openings that stand alone', 'Cutting empty warm-ups' ), 'Generate five subject lines for one email and keep the one a busy reader would understand in two seconds.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Body structure: context, ask, details', 'Use a three-block body so the reader can skim.', 'You will force every draft into context, ask, and supporting details. Then you will ask the model to shorten by 30% without losing the ask. Length is a courtesy. If a detail is needed, it goes below the ask, not above it.', array( 'Context / ask / details', 'Shortening without losing the ask', 'Skimmable paragraphs' ), 'Rewrite one long email into three blocks and cut 30% of the words.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Tone: firm, kind, and still specific', 'Adjust warmth without hiding the request.', 'Tone sliders in a prompt are blunt. You will specify relationship (manager, customer, peer), urgency, and what must stay explicit. Then you will check that “kind” did not delete the deadline. Specific beats soft.', array( 'Relationship and urgency in the prompt', 'Kind without vague', 'Deadlines that survive a rewrite' ), 'Draft the same ask in two tones. Keep the version that is both polite and impossible to misunderstand.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Hard emails: nos, delays, and bad news', 'Use AI for structure, then write the human sentence yourself.', 'Difficult emails need your judgment. You will use the model for outline and phrasing options, then write the actual no, delay, or apology in your voice. Never let the model invent a reason you did not give it. Truth first, polish second.', array( 'Outlines for difficult notes', 'Not inventing reasons', 'Owning the final sentence' ), 'Outline a delay email with AI, then write the real reason in one sentence yourself.' ),
			lac_cl( 'An email prompt library you will actually use', 'Save five templates with blanks for names, dates, and asks.', 'The course sticks if the templates live where you write. You will save five prompts: request, follow-up, intro, decline, and update. Each has blanks. Next week you fill blanks instead of starting from zero.', array( 'Five reusable email prompts', 'Blanks for names and dates', 'Where to store the library' ), 'Save five templates and send one real email from a template today.' ),
		),
		'ai-image-prompts-for-beginners' => array(
			lac_cl( 'How image models read a prompt', 'Describe subject, setting, style, and what to avoid.', 'Image models are not searching a photo library; they are generating from words. You will structure prompts as subject, setting, lighting, style, and negatives. Vague adjectives (“beautiful”) do less than concrete nouns and camera language. This lesson is the grammar.', array( 'Subject, setting, style', 'Lighting and camera cues', 'Negative prompts that help' ), 'Write one prompt with subject, setting, lighting, style, and two things to avoid.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Style without stealing a living artist’s name', 'Use medium, era, and references you are allowed to use.', '“In the style of [living artist]” is a legal and ethical trap. You will specify medium (oil, photo, 3D), era, and public-domain or licensed references instead. You will also note when a client’s brand board should override aesthetic play.', array( 'Medium and era instead of artist names', 'Brand boards versus exploration', 'Licensing awareness' ), 'Rewrite a style-of-artist prompt using only medium, era, and lighting.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Iterate with small changes', 'Change one variable per generation so you learn cause and effect.', 'Beginners rewrite the whole prompt and cannot tell what worked. You will lock the subject and change only lighting, then only crop, then only style. Keep a sheet of what each change did. That sheet is how you get repeatable, not lucky, images.', array( 'One variable per run', 'A change log', 'When to start a new seed' ), 'Generate four versions of one scene, changing only lighting. Note which you would ship.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Composition, text, and hands: known weak spots', 'Plan around what image models still fumble.', 'Text in images, extra fingers, and busy crowds still fail often. You will compose for fewer subjects, avoid small text, and use inpainting or a design tool when words must be readable. Knowing the weak spots is part of prompting skill.', array( 'Few subjects, clear silhouette', 'Avoiding small rendered text', 'When to switch tools' ), 'Design a prompt for a poster where the headline will be added in a design tool, not in the image model.' ),
			lac_cl( 'From prompt to an asset you can use', 'Export, crop, and check resolution for slides, web, or print.', 'A pretty square is not an asset. You will check aspect ratio, resolution, and whether faces or logos are wrong. Then you will crop for the destination. The last 10% is production, not prompting.', array( 'Aspect ratios for real destinations', 'Resolution checks', 'Cropping for the layout' ), 'Create one image and crop it for both a 16:9 slide and a square social post.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A beginner image-prompt library', 'Save ten prompts grouped by job: hero, icon, diagram, mood.', 'You will leave with a small library tagged by use. Next time you start from a tagged prompt, not a blank box. Update the library when a prompt fails twice — that is how it stays honest.', array( 'Prompts grouped by job', 'Tags you can search', 'Retiring prompts that fail' ), 'Save ten prompts with tags. Use one of them for a real slide or post.' ),
		),
		'summarize-documents-with-ai' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What a summary is allowed to do', 'Compress, do not invent, and keep the author’s claims labeled.', 'A summary is a shorter map of a source, not a new article. You will practice staying inside the document, marking uncertainty, and never adding statistics the source did not contain. If you need outside facts, that is a different task with citations.', array( 'Compression versus invention', 'Labeling claims as the author’s', 'When to stop and read the source' ), 'Summarize one page and highlight any sentence you cannot point to in the original.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Choose the summary shape for the reader', 'Executive brief, bullet digest, or action log — not one generic paragraph.', 'Different readers need different shapes. You will produce an executive brief (decisions and risks), a bullet digest (section by section), and an action log (owners and dates). The prompt must name the reader. Shape is the job.', array( 'Executive brief', 'Section digest', 'Action log' ), 'Summarize the same document three ways. Send only the shape your real reader would use.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Chunk long documents without losing the plot', 'Summarize parts, then summarize the summaries with a running thesis.', 'Context windows fill up. You will split by heading, summarize each part, then write a top-level brief from those briefs while keeping a one-line thesis. Watch for contradictions between sections — those belong in the final summary as open issues.', array( 'Splitting on headings', 'Hierarchical summaries', 'Surfacing contradictions' ), 'Split a long doc into three chunks, summarize each, then write a one-page brief from those three.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Tables, numbers, and quotes', 'Keep figures exact and quotes short and attributed.', 'Models round, swap, and invent numbers. You will copy critical figures from the source by hand, ask the model not to invent metrics, and keep quotes short. If a number matters, it is a verify-by-eye step, not a trust-the-draft step.', array( 'Never inventing metrics', 'Short attributed quotes', 'Hand-checking critical numbers' ), 'Extract five numbers from a source into a table and verify each against the original.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A reusable summarization prompt', 'Lock audience, length, and “do not invent” into a template.', 'You will save a template with audience, max length, required sections, and a ban on outside facts. Test it on two different documents. If it drifts, tighten the bans. The template is the product of this course.', array( 'Audience and length in the template', 'Required sections', 'Ban on outside facts' ), 'Save the template and run it on two documents. Note one failure to fix in the prompt.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Hand off a summary others can trust', 'Include source, date, and what you did not cover.', 'A summary without provenance is a rumor. You will add the document title, date, and a “not covered” line. Then you will ask a colleague whether they could act without opening the original. That is the quality bar.', array( 'Source and date', 'What was left out', 'A colleague test' ), 'Send one summary with provenance. Ask the reader if they needed the original anyway, and why.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-social-media-captions' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A caption is a job: hook, value, ask', 'Write for the scroll, then earn the click or comment.', 'Captions fail when they try to be essays. You will use hook, one value, and one ask. Platform limits differ, but the job does not. AI can generate options; you pick the hook that is true.', array( 'Hook, value, ask', 'Truth over cleverness', 'One ask per post' ), 'Write the job for three posts you actually need: hook, value, ask in one line each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Voice and banned phrases for a brand', 'Feed examples so captions sound like the account, not a bot.', 'You will collect five on-brand captions, list words you never use, and put both in the prompt. Then you will generate ten options and kill anything that could be any brand. Consistency is a filter, not an afterthought.', array( 'Example captions', 'Banned phrases', 'Killing generic lines' ), 'Build a one-page voice sheet and generate ten captions. Keep two.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Hooks that match the image or clip', 'Describe the visual so the first line is not a mismatch.', 'The first line has to fit what people see. You will describe the asset in the prompt and ask for hooks that reference it. Mismatch is why AI captions feel fake even when the grammar is fine.', array( 'Describing the asset', 'Hooks tied to the visual', 'Alt text as a bonus' ), 'For one image, write three hooks that only work with that image.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Hashtags, length, and platform shape', 'Match the box: short for some networks, threads for others.', 'You will keep a table of length and hashtag norms, then ask the model for the right shape. Hashtag stuffing is not a strategy. You will pick a small set you actually rank for or skip them.', array( 'Length by platform', 'A small hashtag set', 'When to skip hashtags' ), 'Adapt one caption to two platforms with different length limits.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A week of captions from one idea', 'Batch variations without posting duplicates.', 'You will take one announcement and produce a week of angles: how-to, proof, question, behind-the-scenes. Then you will schedule only the distinct ones. Batching is the time save; duplicates are the tax.', array( 'Angles from one idea', 'Killing near-duplicates', 'A simple calendar' ), 'From one announcement, write seven captions and delete three that say the same thing.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Review before you post', 'Check claims, tags, and whether you would say this out loud.', 'Read every caption aloud. Check names, dates, and claims. AI will happily invent a statistic. This lesson is a two-minute gate so speed does not publish a mistake.', array( 'Aloud test', 'Claim check', 'Names and dates' ), 'Run the gate on five generated captions. Fix or kill each one.' ),
		),
		'build-a-simple-ai-chatbot' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What your chatbot is for', 'Write a one-page job: users, questions, and out-of-scope topics.', 'A chatbot without a job becomes a toy that apologizes. You will name the user, the ten questions it must handle, and the topics it must refuse. That page is the spec. Everything else in the course implements it.', array( 'User and primary questions', 'Out-of-scope list', 'Success in one sentence' ), 'Write the one-page job for a bot you would actually ship.' ),
			lac_cl( 'System prompt and personality without fluff', 'Give rules, tone, and refusal lines — not a novel backstory.', 'You will write a system prompt with role, rules, and canned refusals. Personality is a few constraints (short, plain language), not a fictional childhood. Test it with a rude user and an off-topic user on day one.', array( 'Rules and refusals', 'Tone in one line', 'Tests for rude and off-topic users' ), 'Draft the system prompt and run three adversarial questions. Fix the refusals.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Knowledge: what the bot is allowed to know', 'Ground answers in a small approved set, or say you do not know.', 'Untrained chatbots invent policies. You will attach a short FAQ or docs snippet and instruct the model to answer only from that text. “I don’t know — here’s how to reach a person” is a feature.', array( 'Approved knowledge only', 'Citing the snippet', 'Escalation when knowledge is missing' ), 'Put five facts in a knowledge block and ask a question that is not in it. Confirm the bot refuses.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Conversation state and helpful follow-ups', 'Remember the last goal, not the entire novel.', 'You will keep a short state: topic, user constraint, last unanswered question. Follow-ups should clarify, not stall. This lesson designs the state object and the moment you reset it.', array( 'Minimal conversation state', 'Clarifying questions', 'When to reset' ), 'Define a state object with four fields and write when each field is cleared.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A clickable prototype', 'Wire prompt, knowledge, and a simple UI so someone else can try it.', 'You will connect a single-page UI to your prompt and knowledge. It can be a local script. The point is a URL or a file someone else can click, plus a list of ten test questions.', array( 'A minimal UI', 'Ten test questions', 'Logging one sample transcript' ), 'Stand up the prototype and capture one transcript for a test question that should pass and one that should refuse.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Handoff to a human and next features', 'Decide when the bot stops and who receives the transcript.', 'Every useful bot has an exit. You will define the handoff trigger, the transcript format, and the next two features you will not build yet. Scope is how simple bots stay simple.', array( 'Handoff triggers', 'Transcript for the human', 'A short not-now list' ), 'Write the handoff rule and the email or ticket template the human receives.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-student-research' => array(
			lac_cl( 'AI as a research assistant, not a source', 'Use models to plan, outline, and question — then go to real sources.', 'Student work fails when the chat is cited as evidence. You will use AI to brainstorm search terms, outline questions, and explain jargon, then you will collect sources yourself. The model is a coach. The library is the evidence.', array( 'Planning versus citing', 'Search terms and outlines', 'Why chats are not sources' ), 'For one assignment, generate search terms and questions, then find two real sources without the model.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Reading help: explain, outline, quiz me', 'Turn a hard paper into a study plan you still verify.', 'You will paste a passage you are allowed to use and ask for an outline, a glossary, and five quiz questions. Then you will check the outline against the passage. If a quiz answer is not in the text, throw it out.', array( 'Outlines from a passage', 'Glossary of terms', 'Quiz questions tied to the text' ), 'Create a quiz from one section and mark any question the passage does not answer.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Notes and synthesis across sources', 'Keep source labels so synthesis does not become a blend of rumors.', 'You will summarize source A and source B separately, then ask for a synthesis that keeps labels. Mixed-up citations are the failure mode. Your notes file should still show which claim came from where.', array( 'Per-source notes', 'Labeled synthesis', 'A claims table' ), 'Make a table of five claims with a source column. Do not leave a cell blank.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Drafting with integrity', 'Outline and improve clarity; write the argument in your own words.', 'You will use AI to punch holes in your outline and to simplify sentences you wrote. You will not paste a generated essay as your submission. This lesson includes a practical integrity checklist your course likely already expects.', array( 'Outline critique', 'Editing your own sentences', 'Integrity checklist' ), 'Write a paragraph yourself, then ask only for clarity edits. Keep your argument.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Citations and quotation discipline', 'Quote little, attribute always, and never invent a reference.', 'Models invent papers. You will only cite items you opened. Quotes stay short. If the model suggests a citation, you verify it exists. That is non-negotiable academic practice.', array( 'No invented references', 'Short quotes', 'Verify before you cite' ), 'Take one model-suggested citation and verify it. If it is fake, record how you caught it.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A research session template', 'Repeatable 50-minute blocks: plan, gather, note, question.', 'You will leave with a session template: 10 minutes plan, 25 gather, 10 notes, 5 questions for next time. AI sits in plan and question, not in gather. That split keeps the work honest and faster.', array( 'A 50-minute block', 'Where AI is allowed', 'Questions for the next session' ), 'Run one 50-minute block on a real assignment and fill the template.' ),
		),
		'automate-spreadsheets-with-ai' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What to automate versus what to keep as a formula', 'Use AI for messy text and planning; keep numbers in the sheet.', 'Spreadsheets already calculate. You will use AI for classifying notes, drafting formulas you then test, and explaining errors — not for being the system of record. If a number matters, it lives in a cell with a formula you can audit.', array( 'AI for text and explanations', 'Formulas as source of truth', 'Auditability' ), 'List five spreadsheet tasks. Mark each as formula, AI draft, or human.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Cleaning and classifying columns', 'Turn messy text columns into categories you can filter.', 'You will sample a column, define categories, and have the model label rows — then you will spot-check. Bulk labeling without a check is how bad categories enter a report. Keep an “other” bucket.', array( 'Category lists', 'Spot-checking labels', 'An other bucket' ), 'Classify 20 messy cells into a small category set. Fix the disagreements by hand.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Drafting formulas and checking them', 'Ask for a formula, then test it on known rows.', 'You will describe the rule in English, get a formula, and test on three rows you already know. If it fails, you paste the error, not a new chat. Never paste a formula you do not understand into a whole column.', array( 'English-to-formula', 'Tests on known rows', 'Reading the formula aloud' ), 'Draft one formula with AI, test three rows, and write what the formula does in one sentence.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Explaining errors and circular references', 'Paste the error and the formula, not the whole workbook.', 'You will practice debugging with a minimal repro: formula, sample values, error text. The model is a second pair of eyes. You still decide whether to take the fix. This lesson builds a debug prompt you can reuse.', array( 'Minimal repro', 'Error text in the prompt', 'Accepting fixes you understand' ), 'Take one real spreadsheet error and get a fix you can explain without the chat open.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A repeatable weekly sheet workflow', 'Same steps every week: import, clean, check, summarize.', 'Automation is a ritual. You will write a weekly runbook: where data comes from, which columns AI may touch, which charts you refresh, and who receives the summary. Rituals beat one-off heroics.', array( 'A weekly runbook', 'Columns AI may touch', 'Who gets the summary' ), 'Write the runbook for one sheet you own and run it once.' ),
			lac_cl( 'When to leave the spreadsheet', 'Know when the job belongs in a script, a database, or a proper app.', 'Sheets collapse under size, permissions, and concurrent edits. This lesson names the signals to move. You will not migrate in this course; you will recognize when AI-in-sheets is the wrong long-term home.', array( 'Scale and permission signals', 'What a script buys you', 'A not-yet migration note' ), 'Write three signals that would make you move your sheet, and the first tool you would consider.' ),
		),
		'midjourney-starter-guide' => array(
			lac_cl( 'How Midjourney turns words into images', 'Prompt grammar, aspect ratios, and what the model emphasizes.', 'You will learn how Midjourney weights early words, how `--ar` changes composition, and why style words fight subject words when both are vague. The first skill is a prompt you could hand to a colleague and get a similar idea, not a lottery ticket.', array( 'Prompt order and emphasis', 'Aspect ratios', 'Subject versus style conflict' ), 'Write one prompt two ways (subject-first and style-first) and compare which you would keep.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Parameters you will actually use', 'Stylize, chaos, weird, and seed — in plain language.', 'You will change one parameter at a time on a locked prompt so you can see the effect. Seeds help you iterate a favorite. Chaos is for exploration, not for client work. This lesson is a parameter cheat sheet you fill with your own examples.', array( 'Stylize and chaos', 'Seeds for iteration', 'When not to use weird' ), 'Lock a prompt and run three stylize values. Capture which you would send to a client.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Variations, upscales, and a simple iteration loop', 'Explore, then commit, then refine — in that order.', 'You will use variations to explore, pick one direction, upscale, then change one instruction. Mixing all three at once is how beginners get lost. The loop is the craft.', array( 'Explore then commit', 'One change per refine', 'When to start a new prompt' ), 'Take one image through vary → pick → upscale → one refine. Save the chain.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Consistent characters and products', 'Describe anchors that survive a new scene.', 'Consistency comes from repeating concrete anchors: age, clothing, colors, camera. You will build a character sheet prompt and reuse it in two scenes. Perfect identity is not guaranteed; this lesson is about raising the odds.', array( 'Anchor details', 'A reusable character block', 'Two-scene test' ), 'Write a character block and generate the same character in two settings.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Commercial use, references, and taste', 'Know what you can ship and when to use your own references.', 'You will review the practical issues: likeness, trademarks, and using your own moodboards. Taste is choosing among options, not generating 200 and hoping. This lesson is judgment plus a light legal awareness, not legal advice.', array( 'Likeness and trademarks', 'Your own references', 'Choosing among options' ), 'Build a tiny moodboard of three allowed references and write a prompt that uses them without naming a living artist.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A Midjourney recipe book', 'Save ten recipes by job: product, environment, portrait, diagram-like.', 'You will leave with tagged recipes and the parameters that belong with them. Next project, start from a recipe. Update it when it fails twice.', array( 'Recipes by job', 'Parameters stored with the prompt', 'Retiring failures' ), 'Save ten recipes. Use one on a real brief this week.' ),
		),
		'ai-copywriting-fundamentals' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The job of a piece of copy', 'Promise, proof, and next step — before any generating.', 'You will write the job on a card: who, desire, objection, next step. AI without that card produces fluent mush. This is classic copy discipline, with a model as a fast junior writer you still edit.', array( 'Who and desire', 'Objection', 'Next step' ), 'Fill the job card for one real offer before you open the model.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Headlines: options, then a human pick', 'Generate many, score on clarity and truth, keep two.', 'You will generate twenty headlines, kill clever-but-false, and keep two that a skeptic would accept. Scoring is the skill. Volume is cheap; selection is the work.', array( 'Volume then filter', 'Truth over clever', 'A skeptic test' ), 'Generate twenty headlines. Keep two. Write why the rest failed.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Body copy: PAS, features-to-benefits, and specificity', 'Force structure so paragraphs do a job.', 'You will run Problem-Agitate-Solve and a feature-to-benefit pass, then replace adjectives with specifics you provide. The model cannot invent your proof. You paste proof or the copy stays generic.', array( 'PAS structure', 'Features to benefits', 'Specifics you supply' ), 'Write one section with PAS and swap three vague adjectives for specifics from the real offer.' ),
			lac_cl( 'CTAs and microcopy', 'Make the next step obvious and honest.', 'You will draft buttons, form hints, and error text. Honest CTAs beat fake urgency. Ask the model for options, then pick the one that matches what actually happens after the click.', array( 'Button language', 'Form and error microcopy', 'No fake urgency' ), 'Write three CTAs for one page. Keep the one that matches the real next step.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Voice sheets for campaigns', 'Lock examples, reading level, and words you refuse.', 'A voice sheet travels between ads, landing pages, and emails. You will build one page: examples, reading level, banned claims. Every prompt in the campaign includes it. That is how a team stays consistent with AI in the mix.', array( 'Examples and reading level', 'Banned claims', 'Including the sheet in every prompt' ), 'Create the voice sheet and generate one ad and one email from it.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Edit like a copy chief', 'Cut, check claims, and read aloud before anything ships.', 'You will run a chief pass: cut 20%, verify every claim, read aloud. AI drafts are first drafts. This lesson is the standard that makes the rest of the course safe to use at work.', array( 'Cut 20%', 'Claim verification', 'Aloud test' ), 'Take one generated landing section through the chief pass and paste before/after.' ),
		),
		'prompt-engineering-essentials' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A prompt is a spec', 'Requirements, examples, and tests — not poetry.', 'You will treat prompts like small specs: inputs, outputs, constraints, and examples. This mindset is the jump from casual chat to engineering. If you cannot test it, it is not engineered yet.', array( 'Inputs and outputs', 'Constraints', 'Test cases' ), 'Write a spec for one prompt: inputs, output schema, three tests.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Patterns: role, few-shot, and chain of steps', 'Use patterns on purpose, not as superstition.', 'You will apply role only when it changes behavior, few-shot when format is fussy, and step lists when the task has phases. You will also see when a pattern adds tokens without adding quality. Measurement beats folklore.', array( 'When role helps', 'Few-shot for format', 'Steps for multi-phase tasks' ), 'Take one task and try two patterns. Keep the cheaper one if quality is equal.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Delimiter, context packing, and instruction order', 'Put the instruction where the model will not bury it.', 'You will wrap untrusted text, put the task last or clearly marked, and pack only relevant context. This lesson prevents prompt injection lite and “lost in the middle” on longer inputs. Structure is a reliability tool.', array( 'Delimiters for pasted text', 'Instruction placement', 'Relevant context only' ), 'Rewrite a messy prompt with delimiters and a clear task block.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Eval: a tiny suite you rerun after every change', 'Five to twenty examples beat a vibe check.', 'You will build a fixture file of inputs and expected traits. After each prompt change, you rerun. This is the essential practice that separates tinkering from engineering.', array( 'Fixture examples', 'Expected traits', 'Rerun after every change' ), 'Create ten fixtures for your spec and score a prompt against them.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Failure modes and repairs', 'Refusal, ramble, schema break, and sycophancy — with a fix for each.', 'You will keep a repair card: if ramble, add length and format; if schema break, add validation; if sycophancy, ask for critique. Engineers collect failures. This lesson starts your card.', array( 'Common failure modes', 'A repair card', 'When to change the model instead' ), 'Log three real failures and the repair you used.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Versioning prompts like code', 'Name, date, owner, and what the last eval said.', 'A prompt without a version is a production incident waiting. You will store prompts with a changelog and the eval score. That is essential even for a one-person team.', array( 'Names and owners', 'Changelog', 'Eval score beside the prompt' ), 'Version your best prompt v1 and note the eval score and date.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-customer-support' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The support jobs AI should take first', 'Deflection, drafting, and search — not unsupervised refunds.', 'You will map ticket types: FAQ, how-to, billing curiosity, angry, legal. AI drafts and suggests; policies and money stay human until you have evals. This lesson is the triage map.', array( 'Ticket types', 'Draft versus decide', 'A first-wave scope' ), 'Label 20 real or sample tickets as AI-draft, AI-search, or human-only.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Grounding answers in help center text', 'Answer from approved articles or escalate.', 'You will retrieve the right article, quote it, and refuse when nothing matches. Hallucinated policies are more expensive than a slow ticket. Grounding is the product.', array( 'Retrieve then answer', 'Quotes from articles', 'Escalate on miss' ), 'Take three questions: two in the help center, one not. Confirm the third escalates.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Drafting replies in the brand voice', 'Empathy plus the next step, without fake intimacy.', 'You will use macros plus AI to draft, then require an agent to send. Voice sheets and banned promises live in the prompt. “I understand” without a next step is not support.', array( 'Voice and banned promises', 'Next step in every draft', 'Agent send' ), 'Draft three replies from one article. Keep the one with a clear next step.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Macros, tags, and the queue', 'Use AI to suggest tags and summaries for humans, not to hide tickets.', 'Summaries help the next agent. Suggested tags help reporting. You will design suggestions that a human confirms. Automation that hides a VIP ticket is a failure.', array( 'Suggested tags', 'Ticket summaries', 'Human confirm' ), 'Design the suggested-tag list and the summary fields the next agent needs.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Quality and safety in support AI', 'PII, threats, and medical or legal overreach.', 'You will list data that must not go to a model, how you redact, and when the bot must stop. Safety here is operational, not theoretical.', array( 'PII redaction', 'Stop topics', 'Audit samples' ), 'Write the redaction rules and three stop topics for your queue.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A 30-day support AI rollout', 'Start with drafts on one queue, measure handle time and reopen rate.', 'You will pick one queue, one metric pair, and a weekly sample review. Rollout is a process. This lesson writes the plan.', array( 'One queue', 'Handle time and reopens', 'Weekly sample review' ), 'Write the 30-day plan with owners and the two metrics you will watch.' ),
		),
		'create-ai-powered-faqs' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Mine real questions, not imagined ones', 'Build the FAQ from tickets, chats, and search logs.', 'You will cluster real questions before you write answers. An FAQ invented in a workshop will miss what people type. This lesson is the source list.', array( 'Tickets and search logs', 'Clustering questions', 'Frequency versus risk' ), 'Collect 30 real questions and group them into at most 12 FAQ entries.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Draft answers from approved sources', 'One source of truth per answer, then simplify.', 'You will attach the policy or doc, draft a short answer, and keep the source link. AI simplifies; it does not become the policy. Conflicts go back to the owner of the doc.', array( 'One source per answer', 'Short answers', 'Owner for conflicts' ), 'Draft five FAQ answers with a source link on each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Question variants people actually type', 'Cover paraphrases, typos, and the angry version.', 'You will generate variants for search and for a bot, then have a human keep the ones that sound real. This is how FAQs become findable.', array( 'Paraphrases', 'Tone variants', 'Human filter' ), 'For one FAQ, write eight variants and keep the five a customer would type.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Structure for web, bot, and search', 'Same truth, different length and schema.', 'You will keep a canonical answer and derive a 40-word bot version and an SEO-friendly title. One truth, three shapes. Drift is the enemy.', array( 'Canonical answer', 'Bot-length version', 'Titles for search' ), 'Publish one FAQ in three shapes from the same canonical text.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Review cadence and stale answers', 'A FAQ is a product with an owner and a date.', 'You will assign owners, review dates, and a trigger (policy change). Stale FAQs destroy trust faster than missing ones. This lesson is the operating system.', array( 'Owners and dates', 'Policy-change triggers', 'Retiring entries' ), 'Add owner and review date to your FAQ list. Retire one stale item.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Measure whether the FAQ is working', 'Watch search misses, ticket deflection, and thumbs down.', 'You will pick two metrics and a monthly read. If people still ticket the same question, the answer is wrong or unfindable. Measurement closes the loop.', array( 'Search misses', 'Deflection', 'Thumbs down' ), 'Define two metrics and where you will read them each month.' ),
		),
		'ai-video-script-writing' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A script is timed speech, not a blog post', 'Write for breath, picture, and a clock.', 'You will mark seconds, visual cues, and spoken lines separately. AI drafts walls of text; you cut them into breaths. This lesson is the script template.', array( 'Time, picture, line', 'Breath-length sentences', 'A script template' ), 'Fill the template for a 60-second video: time, visual, line.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Hook, promise, and payoff in the first 15 seconds', 'Earn the watch before you teach.', 'You will generate hook options, pick one that is true, and land a promise the rest of the script keeps. If the payoff is missing, the hook is clickbait. Structure before style.', array( 'True hooks', 'A keepable promise', 'Payoff placement' ), 'Write three hooks for one video and the payoff sentence that makes each honest.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Explain on camera without dumping slides', 'One idea per beat, with a visual verb.', 'You will break a topic into beats, each with one idea and something to show. AI can propose beats; you sequence them. Talking heads need visual verbs.', array( 'Beats', 'One idea each', 'Visual verbs' ), 'Turn one topic into six beats with a visual for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Voiceover versus on-camera: two prompt modes', 'Match the prompt to delivery.', 'VO can be denser; on-camera needs shorter lines and room to gesture. You will keep two prompt templates. Mixing them makes robotic talking-head scripts.', array( 'VO density', 'On-camera shortness', 'Two templates' ), 'Write the same 30 seconds for VO and for on-camera. Read both aloud.' ),
			lac_cl( 'CTA and end cards that match the video', 'Ask for one action the viewer can do immediately.', 'You will place a single CTA, say it, and show it. AI loves three CTAs. You will pick one. End cards are part of the script, not an afterthought.', array( 'One CTA', 'Said and shown', 'End card as script' ), 'Add a one-action close to your 60-second script.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A revision pass for rhythm', 'Cut 15%, mark stresses, and time it with a stopwatch.', 'You will read with a timer, cut, and only then ask AI to suggest tighter lines you still approve. Rhythm is human. This is the last gate before record.', array( 'Stopwatch', 'Cut 15%', 'Stress marks' ), 'Time your script, cut 15%, and record a voice memo of the result.' ),
		),
		'fine-tune-your-ai-workflow' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Map the workflow you already have', 'Boxes, handoffs, and where AI is already sneaking in.', 'You will sketch the current path from request to done. Hidden ChatGPT use is still part of the map. You cannot tune what you have not drawn.', array( 'Boxes and handoffs', 'Hidden AI use', 'Cycle time today' ), 'Draw the current workflow and mark every AI touch, official or not.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Pick one bottleneck worth an AI wedge', 'Highest pain that is still a drafting or matching job.', 'You will score steps on pain, volume, and whether the job is generation or judgment. Wedge the first, not the flashiest. This lesson picks the pilot.', array( 'Pain and volume', 'Generation versus judgment', 'The pilot step' ), 'Score four steps and name the one-week pilot.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Design the wedge: trigger, prompt, output home', 'Make AI a step with a door in and a door out.', 'You will specify what starts it, the prompt, and where the output lands (doc, ticket, sheet). Orphan drafts in a chat history are not a workflow. This is the design.', array( 'Trigger', 'Prompt', 'Output home' ), 'Write trigger, prompt name, and output home for the pilot.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Quality gates in the path', 'A human check where mistakes are expensive.', 'You will place a gate: who checks, what they check, how long it may take. Gates are how you tune speed without silent errors.', array( 'Who checks', 'What they check', 'Time budget for the gate' ), 'Add one gate to the pilot and a two-item checklist for the reviewer.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Measure the wedge', 'Time, rework, and whether people skip the step.', 'You will baseline last week’s time, then compare. If people skip the AI step, the output home is wrong or the prompt is slower than the old way. Measurement tells you which.', array( 'Baseline time', 'Rework', 'Skip rate' ), 'Capture a one-week baseline and the three numbers you will compare.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Tune weekly, then clone', 'Change one variable a week; copy only what worked.', 'You will keep a changelog. After two good weeks, you clone the wedge to a similar step. Tuning is a calendar habit. This lesson writes the habit.', array( 'One change per week', 'Changelog', 'Clone rules' ), 'Schedule four weekly tune sessions and the clone candidate if the pilot works.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-e-commerce-product-descriptions' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Facts first: a product spec sheet the model may use', 'SKU data, materials, and claims you can defend.', 'You will build the spec the model is allowed to see. Invented materials and fake certifications are how shops get in trouble. Facts live in the sheet; poetry lives in the draft.', array( 'Allowed facts', 'Banned claims', 'The spec sheet' ), 'Fill a spec sheet for one SKU including what you must not say.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Description structure that converts and informs', 'Hook, facts, fit, care, and proof — in that order.', 'You will lock a block order so every SKU is scannable. AI fills blocks from the spec. If a block has no facts, it stays empty. Empty is better than fiction.', array( 'Block order', 'Empty over fiction', 'Scannable SKUs' ), 'Write the block template and fill it for one product without adding new facts.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Variants, SEO titles, and bullets', 'Same spec, different lengths, no keyword stuffing.', 'You will generate title, bullets, and long description from one spec. Keywords only if they are true. Variants (size, color) inherit facts, not fluff.', array( 'Title, bullets, long form', 'True keywords only', 'Variant inheritance' ), 'Produce title, five bullets, and a long description from one spec.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Voice for the catalog', 'One brand sheet so 500 SKUs do not sound like 500 interns.', 'You will lock reading level, humor, and words you never use. Batch generation without a voice sheet is how catalogs get weird. Include the sheet in every prompt.', array( 'Reading level', 'Banned words', 'Batch consistency' ), 'Create the voice sheet and generate two SKUs. They should sound related.' ),
			lac_cl( 'QA: claims, sizes, and forbidden medical language', 'A sampling process before publish.', 'You will sample SKUs, check measurements, and search for banned phrases. QA is a queue, not a hope. This lesson writes the sample checklist.', array( 'Measurement checks', 'Banned phrase search', 'Sample size' ), 'Run the checklist on ten generated descriptions. Log defects.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A publishing pipeline', 'Draft, QA, publish, and refresh when specs change.', 'When the spec changes, the copy must change. You will define the trigger and the owner. A pipeline keeps AI copy from rotting on the PDP.', array( 'Draft to QA to live', 'Spec-change trigger', 'Owner' ), 'Write the pipeline with owners and the refresh trigger.' ),
		),
		'build-ai-forms-that-convert' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The form job and the drop-off math', 'Fewer fields, clearer why, AI only where it reduces friction.', 'You will map the form goal and the cost of each field. AI can draft helper text and classify open answers; it should not add mystery fields. Conversion is subtraction plus trust.', array( 'Goal of the form', 'Cost of each field', 'Where AI helps' ), 'List every field and whether it is required for the job. Cut two.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Helper text and error copy that unblock', 'Explain the why and the fix, not just “invalid.”', 'You will generate helper text from the field’s job and error copy that says how to fix. Tone stays calm. This is microcopy work with a model as a first draft.', array( 'Why this field exists', 'Fix-oriented errors', 'Calm tone' ), 'Rewrite helper and error text for three fields.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Open text that you can route', 'Classify intent, then send to the right queue or FAQ.', 'You will design categories, examples, and a fallback. AI classification without a fallback pile is how tickets vanish. Humans still see the original text.', array( 'Intent categories', 'Examples', 'Fallback pile' ), 'Define six intents and three examples each, plus the fallback.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Prefill and assist without creepy', 'Use known data you are allowed to have, and say so.', 'You will decide what can be prefilled and how you disclose it. Suggesting a company name from an email domain can be helpful or creepy. Policy first.', array( 'Allowed prefill', 'Disclosure', 'Creepy versus helpful' ), 'Write the prefill policy for one form in five bullets.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Accessibility and mobile with AI drafts', 'Check labels, order, and tap targets; do not trust the draft layout.', 'AI will happily produce inaccessible copy (“click here”). You will keep visible labels, logical order, and no placeholder-only fields. Then you test on a phone.', array( 'Visible labels', 'Order', 'Phone test' ), 'Audit one form against the three rules and fix what the draft got wrong.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Measure and iterate the form', 'Completion, time, and error rate — then one change a week.', 'You will baseline, ship helper-text changes, and watch error rate. Forms are experiments. This lesson is the cadence.', array( 'Completion and errors', 'One change a week', 'Cadence' ), 'Write the dashboard of three metrics and the first experiment.' ),
		),
		'ai-translation-localization' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Translation versus localization', 'Meaning, then culture, then UI length.', 'You will separate literal translation from localization: examples, humor, units, and legal. AI is a first pass. A reviewer who knows the market still owns the release. This lesson is the brief.', array( 'Meaning versus culture', 'Units and legal', 'Reviewer owns release' ), 'Take one English string and note what must change for a market you care about besides words.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A glossary and do-not-translate list', 'Product names, placeholders, and terms that stay English.', 'You will build a glossary the model must obey and a list of placeholders like `{name}` that must survive. Most AI translation bugs are broken placeholders and inconsistent product names.', array( 'Glossary', 'Placeholders', 'Product names' ), 'Create a 15-term glossary and five do-not-translate strings.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Prompting for a first pass', 'Audience, formality, and “keep placeholders.”', 'You will lock formality, audience, and glossary in the prompt. Then you translate a small pack, not a whole app. Pack size is how you find glossary bugs early.', array( 'Formality', 'Glossary in the prompt', 'Small packs' ), 'Translate a 20-string pack with the glossary and check every placeholder.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Review: fluency, accuracy, and UI overflow', 'A bilingual pass plus a layout pass.', 'You will check accuracy against source, fluency for the market, and whether buttons still fit. AI will not know your 80-character cap unless you say so. Caps belong in the prompt and in QA.', array( 'Accuracy', 'Fluency', 'Length caps' ), 'Review 20 strings for accuracy, fluency, and length. Log defects.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Tone and inclusive language across locales', 'What is polite in one market is wrong in another.', 'You will document tone per locale and banned jokes. The model defaults to a generic register. Your sheet overrides it. This is localization, not decoration.', array( 'Tone per locale', 'Banned jokes', 'Override the default register' ), 'Write a tone sheet for two locales and regenerate one marketing paragraph for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A localization pipeline', 'Source freeze, AI pass, review, ship, and leftover tickets.', 'You will define freeze dates, pack size, and who signs off. Pipeline beats last-minute dumps into chat. This lesson writes the pipeline.', array( 'Freeze', 'Packs', 'Sign-off' ), 'Write the pipeline with dates and roles for one release.' ),
		),
		'retrieval-basics-for-ai-apps' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Why retrieval exists', 'The model does not have your docs unless you fetch them.', 'You will see hallucinations as a missing-retrieval problem. RAG is: find relevant chunks, then generate with those chunks in the prompt. This lesson is the picture on the whiteboard.', array( 'Hallucination as missing context', 'Retrieve then generate', 'What RAG is not' ), 'Explain RAG in four sentences to a non-engineer and draw the boxes.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Chunking without destroying meaning', 'Split on structure, overlap a little, keep citations.', 'You will chunk by heading, keep overlap, and store source ids. Bad chunking is why retrieval “does not work.” This lesson is mechanical and important.', array( 'Structure-aware splits', 'Overlap', 'Source ids' ), 'Chunk one article into 8–12 pieces with source ids you could show a user.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Embeddings and similarity in plain language', 'Close vectors mean similar meaning, not truth.', 'You will embed chunks and a question, then rank. Similar is not correct. You still need the later generate step to follow instructions. This lesson demystifies the math you need.', array( 'What an embedding is', 'Ranking chunks', 'Similar versus correct' ), 'Rank five chunks for one question by hand after reading them. Then compare to a tool’s ranking if you have one.' ),
			lac_cl( 'The generate step with citations', 'Answer only from retrieved text and show sources.', 'You will prompt: use only these snippets, quote ids, say you do not know. Citations are how users trust the app. This is the contract.', array( 'Only these snippets', 'Citation ids', 'I do not know' ), 'Write the generate prompt and produce one answered and one refused example.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Failure modes: wrong chunk, empty, and conflict', 'What users should see in each case.', 'You will design empty states, conflict flags when two docs disagree, and a “wrong chunk” feedback button. Retrieval apps need product design, not just a vector DB.', array( 'Empty', 'Conflicts', 'Feedback' ), 'Write UI copy for empty, conflict, and “this wasn’t relevant.”' ),
			lac_cl( 'A smallest useful retrieval demo', 'Ten docs, one question box, citations on screen.', 'You will outline a vertical slice. It can be local. The point is to feel retrieve-then-generate end to end with citations. That is the basics, complete.', array( 'Ten docs', 'One box', 'Citations on screen' ), 'Storyboard the demo screens and the data you need to load.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-hr-recruiting' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Where AI belongs in hiring — and where it does not', 'Drafting and organizing versus deciding who gets the job.', 'You will map sourcing, screening, scheduling, and offers. AI can draft and summarize. Hiring decisions and protected-class judgments stay human, with your counsel’s rules. This is the ethical and practical frame.', array( 'Hiring stages', 'Draft versus decide', 'Human-owned decisions' ), 'Label each hiring stage as AI-draft, AI-organize, or human-only for your org.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Job descriptions that attract and stay accurate', 'Must-haves versus nice-to-haves, without inflated requirements.', 'You will generate JD options from a role brief, then cut requirements you do not actually test. Inflated JDs waste everyone’s time. AI will happily make a role sound like three jobs. You will not.', array( 'Role brief', 'Must versus nice', 'Cutting inflation' ), 'Rewrite one JD with a must-have list you could defend in an interview plan.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Sourcing messages that are specific', 'Why this person, why this role, one clear ask.', 'You will personalize from public professional facts you are allowed to use, not from creepy data. Specific beats flattery. This lesson is a message template with blanks.', array( 'Allowed facts', 'Why this role', 'One ask' ), 'Write three sourcing notes that mention a real, allowed detail and one ask.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Interview kits and scorecards', 'Questions tied to the must-haves, plus a rubric.', 'You will generate questions from the must-haves and a 1–5 rubric. Interviewers still score. AI must not score candidates from a resume in ways your policy forbids. Kits create consistency.', array( 'Questions from must-haves', 'Rubric', 'Human scoring' ), 'Build a one-role kit: five questions and a rubric.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Summaries after interviews', 'Capture evidence, not vibes, and keep the notes auditable.', 'You will turn interviewer notes into a summary with quotes and examples. You will not let the model add a hire/no-hire the interviewer did not state. Evidence first.', array( 'Evidence and quotes', 'No invented decisions', 'Auditability' ), 'Turn a messy note into a summary with three evidence bullets. No recommendation unless it was in the notes.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A compliant-enough operating rhythm', 'Document purpose, vendors, and human review. Get legal review before you scale.', 'You will list what data goes to which tool and who reviews outputs. This is not legal advice. It is the homework your counsel will ask for. Write it down.', array( 'Data map', 'Human review', 'Counsel before scale' ), 'Fill a one-page data map for your recruiting AI uses.' ),
		),
		'voice-ai-text-to-speech' => array(
			lac_cl( 'When voice is the right interface', 'Hands-busy, accessibility, and brand — versus a form.', 'You will pick use cases where speaking is faster or more accessible, and skip voice where privacy or noise makes it worse. Voice is a product choice. This lesson is the fit test.', array( 'Fit test', 'Privacy and noise', 'When a form wins' ), 'Score three product ideas for voice fit. Kill one.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Write for the ear', 'Shorter sentences, pronounceable names, no tables.', 'You will rewrite UI copy as spoken copy. SSML or pause marks come after the sentences work. AI can draft; you read it aloud. If you stumble, the user will too.', array( 'Ear-first sentences', 'Names and numbers', 'Aloud test' ), 'Rewrite a settings screen as spoken copy and time it.' ),
			lac_cl( 'TTS: voice, speed, and emphasis', 'Pick a voice that matches the job, then lock speed.', 'You will compare voices on the same script, set a default speed, and mark a few emphases. Random default voices make a product feel unfinished. Lock the brand voice.', array( 'Voice choice', 'Speed', 'Emphasis' ), 'Pick one voice and speed for a product script and justify it in three bullets.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Speech-to-text and messy audio', 'Expect errors; design confirmations for high-stakes fields.', 'You will plan for misheard numbers and names. Confirm critical fields. Whisper-quiet rooms are not your users’ rooms. The design includes the error.', array( 'Expected STT errors', 'Confirmations', 'Noisy environments' ), 'Design a confirmation for a spoken phone number or amount.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A simple voice flow', 'Intent, one slot, confirm, done — with a way out.', 'You will storyboard a four-turn flow plus “talk to a person.” Barge-in and silence timeouts are part of the script. Keep it small enough to test with three colleagues.', array( 'Four turns', 'A way out', 'Timeouts' ), 'Storyboard the flow including silence and “agent please.”' ),
			lac_cl( 'Ship checklist for voice', 'Consent, recording policy, and a fallback UI.', 'You will note consent, whether you store audio, and the visual fallback. Voice without a fallback fails accessibility and noisy rooms. This is the ship bar.', array( 'Consent', 'Storage', 'Fallback UI' ), 'Write the ship checklist for your flow in one page.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-marketing-analytics' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Questions before dashboards', 'Name the decision, the metric, and the grain.', 'You will refuse to “analyze the data” without a decision. Grain (day, campaign, country) matters. AI is good at proposing cuts; you own the question. This lesson writes three decision questions.', array( 'Decision', 'Metric', 'Grain' ), 'Write three questions in the form: If we knew X at grain Y, we would do Z.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Prepare a model-safe table', 'Definitions, date ranges, and what the columns mean.', 'You will attach a data dictionary. Models misread “sessions” versus “users.” The dictionary is the prompt. Garbage columns in, confident nonsense out.', array( 'Dictionary', 'Date range', 'Column meanings' ), 'Write a dictionary for one export you actually use.' ),
			lac_cl( 'First-pass analysis you can check', 'Trends, mix shifts, and anomalies — then verify in the sheet.', 'You will ask for anomalies and mix shifts, then click into the sheet. AI is a scout, not the system of record. If a number is surprising, you recompute it.', array( 'Anomalies', 'Mix shifts', 'Verify in source' ), 'Get a first-pass narrative and verify two numbers by hand.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Narratives for stakeholders', 'What happened, why we think so, what we will do, what we will watch.', 'You will force a four-part narrative. Ban fake causality. “We think, because” is required. This is how analytics AI becomes a memo, not a vibe.', array( 'Four-part memo', 'No fake causality', 'Watch list' ), 'Write the memo for one week of data with two verified numbers.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Experiments and incrementality caution', 'Do not let a model declare a winner from correlated charts.', 'You will list what would be needed for a causal claim and keep AI in the role of explaining the experiment design, not inventing significance. Marketers get burned here. Stay humble.', array( 'Correlation versus lift', 'Experiment design help', 'No invented p-values' ), 'Take one “the campaign worked” claim and rewrite it as correlation plus what an experiment would need.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A weekly analytics ritual', 'Same export, same questions, same memo shape every Monday.', 'Ritual beats a heroic one-off. You will lock the export, the three questions, and the memo template. AI fills the first draft; you verify. That is the operating system.', array( 'Locked export', 'Three questions', 'Memo template' ), 'Put the ritual on the calendar with the three questions attached.' ),
		),
		'custom-gpts-for-your-business' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A custom GPT is a product with a job', 'One user, one job, one success test.', 'You will write the job before you click create. Multi-job GPTs become confused interns. Success is a test question with a good answer. This is the brief.', array( 'One job', 'One user', 'A test question' ), 'Write the brief and the test question for a GPT you would give a teammate.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Instructions that behave like a policy', 'Rules, tone, tools, and refusals in one document.', 'You will write instructions as numbered rules. Include what to do when knowledge is missing. Personality is last. Policy is first. This document is the GPT.', array( 'Numbered rules', 'Missing knowledge', 'Tone last' ), 'Draft the instruction doc and run the test question plus one off-limits question.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Knowledge files that stay true', 'Small, dated, and owned — not a junk drawer of PDFs.', 'You will upload a curated pack with dates. Conflicting files make confident wrong answers. An owner updates the pack. This lesson is knowledge hygiene.', array( 'Curated pack', 'Dates', 'Owner' ), 'List the files you would upload and the owner for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Actions and tools with least privilege', 'Only the APIs the job needs, with confirmation for writes.', 'You will map read versus write actions. Writes need confirmation. A GPT with a wide tool belt will use it. Least privilege is the design.', array( 'Read versus write', 'Confirmation', 'Least privilege' ), 'List actions for your GPT. Mark writes as confirm-required.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Eval and a feedback loop', 'Ten questions, expected answers, and a weekly review.', 'You will keep a tiny eval set. When users complain, you add a case. Instructions change only after eval. That is how custom GPTs stay useful.', array( 'Ten questions', 'Weekly review', 'Change after eval' ), 'Write ten eval questions and the expected traits of a good answer.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Rollout inside the company', 'Who can use it, how to start, and when to retire it.', 'You will write a launch note, a starter example, and a retirement rule (unused for 60 days). Internal GPTs rot. Rollout includes the end of life.', array( 'Launch note', 'Starter example', 'Retirement' ), 'Write the launch note and the retirement rule.' ),
		),
		'ai-content-calendar-automation' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A calendar is a system, not a spreadsheet of wishes', 'Themes, cadence, and owners before any generating.', 'You will lock themes for the month, cadence per channel, and who approves. AI fills slots; it does not set strategy. Empty strategy plus AI is a content firehose. This lesson is the system.', array( 'Themes', 'Cadence', 'Approver' ), 'Fill a one-month theme board with cadence and an approver.' ),
			lac_cl( 'From theme to a week of distinct angles', 'Batch ideas, then kill duplicates.', 'You will generate angles from a theme and keep only distinct jobs (teach, prove, invite). Duplicates waste the calendar. Human taste is the filter.', array( 'Angles from a theme', 'Distinct jobs', 'Kill duplicates' ), 'From one theme, produce 12 angles and keep 6.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Briefs the creators can shoot or write from', 'Asset type, hook, proof, CTA, and due date.', 'You will generate briefs, not finished posts, unless the channel is low-stakes. Briefs are how automation helps a team. Missing proof in the brief becomes empty posts later.', array( 'Asset type', 'Proof', 'Due date' ), 'Write four briefs for next week with proof and due dates.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Drafts where they belong', 'Put copy in the tool the team already uses.', 'You will send drafts to the calendar tool or doc, not leave them in chat. Handoff is the automation. This lesson maps fields.', array( 'Field map', 'No drafts in chat', 'Status: draft vs approved' ), 'Map brief fields to your calendar tool, even if the map is a table in a doc.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Approval and legal review in the path', 'A gate with a SLA, especially for claims.', 'You will place approval before scheduled. Claims about products or results get a legal or owner look. Speed without a gate is how brands get hurt.', array( 'Approval SLA', 'Claims gate', 'Scheduled only after' ), 'Write the gate and the SLA for one channel.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A monthly retro on the calendar', 'What got skipped, what performed, what to stop generating.', 'You will look at skip rate and performance, then retire themes. Automation should shrink. This lesson is the retro agenda.', array( 'Skip rate', 'Performance', 'Retire themes' ), 'Write a 30-minute retro agenda with three questions.' ),
		),
		'embeddings-explained-simply' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What an embedding is in one picture', 'A list of numbers that puts similar meanings nearby.', 'You will drop the mystery: text in, vector out, similar meaning closer together. You will also hear what embeddings are not (not a truth database). This is the intuition lesson.', array( 'Text to vector', 'Nearby means similar', 'Not a knowledge base' ), 'Explain embeddings to a colleague in under a minute using one analogy you actually like.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Similarity, nearest neighbors, and ranking', 'A question vector versus document vectors.', 'You will rank a toy set by hand conceptually, then talk cosine similarity without the exam. Ranking is the product primitive. Everything else is engineering around it.', array( 'Query versus docs', 'Ranking', 'Cosine in plain language' ), 'Rank four short sentences against a question without a computer, then say why you ordered them that way.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Where embeddings show up in products', 'Search, clustering, recommendations, RAG.', 'You will map product features to the primitive. If you cannot name the primitive, you are not ready to add a vendor. This lesson connects the math to the roadmap.', array( 'Search', 'Clustering', 'RAG' ), 'Pick one product feature and write which embedding job it is.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Chunking and metadata — the practical bits', 'What you embed, and what you store beside it.', 'You will choose chunk size conceptually and which metadata you must keep (source, date). Bad metadata makes great vectors useless in the UI. Practical beats theoretical.', array( 'What to embed', 'Metadata', 'Source and date' ), 'For one doc type, write chunking rules and required metadata.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Limits: antonyms, recency, and multilingual', 'Nearby is not opposite-aware or automatically up to date.', 'You will learn failure modes: “hot” vs “cold”, old docs ranking well, language mismatch. You will design around them (filters, dates, language). Knowing limits is the point of “explained simply.”', array( 'Not opposite-aware', 'Recency filters', 'Language' ), 'Write three failure examples and a product fix for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A tiny mental demo you could build later', 'Ten sentences, one query, show top three.', 'You will specify the demo so the idea is concrete. No code required in this course, but the spec should be buildable. That is completion.', array( 'Ten sentences', 'Top three', 'Show the text not the vector' ), 'Write the demo spec and the three user-visible fields.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-code-review' => array(
			lac_cl( 'What AI review is good at', 'Nits, missing tests, and “explain this diff” — not architecture sign-off.', 'You will set the role: AI as a noisy intern reviewer. Humans still own merge. This lesson is the policy for your team, even if the team is you.', array( 'Nits and tests', 'Explain the diff', 'Human merge' ), 'Write a three-bullet policy for AI in your review process.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Prompts that review a diff, not the whole repo', 'Paste the diff, the standard, and the risk areas.', 'You will include coding standards and the files that matter. Whole-repo dumps waste context. Ask for severity and suggested tests. This is the review prompt.', array( 'Diff plus standard', 'Severity', 'Suggested tests' ), 'Run a review prompt on a small real diff and keep only comments you agree with.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Security and secrets in the loop', 'Never paste secrets; ask for OWASP-ish checks you then verify.', 'You will redact, use private models if required, and treat security comments as leads. AI will both miss vulns and invent them. You verify. This lesson is hygiene plus skepticism.', array( 'Redaction', 'Leads not gospel', 'Verify' ), 'Make a redaction checklist for diffs you would send to a model.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Tests the model suggests', 'Turn “add tests” into concrete cases you write.', 'You will ask for cases, then you write them. Suggested tests that you do not add are theater. This lesson closes the loop.', array( 'Concrete cases', 'You write the test', 'No theater' ), 'Take one AI test suggestion and implement or reject it with a reason.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Style noise versus real defects', 'Filter comments so authors do not drown.', 'You will ask the model to separate nits from defects, then you hide nits if your linter already covers them. Reviewer fatigue is a product problem. Configure the prompt.', array( 'Nits versus defects', 'Linter overlap', 'Author fatigue' ), 'Configure a prompt that reports at most five defects and puts nits last.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A team ritual', 'When to invoke AI review and how comments get into the PR.', 'You will decide bot comments versus a human pasting a summary. Rituals beat random use. This lesson writes the ritual.', array( 'When to run', 'How comments appear', 'Who still approves' ), 'Write the ritual in a README section you could paste to a teammate.' ),
		),
		'build-an-ai-writing-assistant' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The assistant’s job and non-goals', 'One writer, one document type, one success test.', 'You will pick a document type (brief, blog, ticket) and a success test. Assistants that do all writing do none well. Non-goals keep the prompt small.', array( 'Document type', 'Success test', 'Non-goals' ), 'Write the job and three non-goals for your assistant.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Commands instead of a blank chat', 'Outline, shorten, change audience, check claims.', 'You will design slash-like commands so users do not invent prompts. Each command has a spec. This is the UX of a writing assistant.', array( 'Named commands', 'A spec per command', 'No blank-page requirement' ), 'Specify four commands with inputs and outputs.' ),
			lac_cl( 'User text as source of truth', 'Transform their words; do not replace their argument.', 'You will prompt to preserve claims and only add structure or clarity. Inventing facts is a product bug. This lesson is the integrity rule.', array( 'Preserve claims', 'Structure and clarity', 'No new facts' ), 'Take a paragraph and run “clarify” without adding a statistic.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Memory and style', 'A short style card the user can edit.', 'You will store a style card (examples, banned words) the user owns. Hidden memory that the user cannot see will betray them. Visible style is the feature.', array( 'Editable style card', 'Examples', 'No hidden memory' ), 'Design the style card fields and an example filled card.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A vertical slice', 'One command, one doc, one UI button.', 'You will wire one command end to end. That is the assistant. More commands come after people use the first. Slice over suite.', array( 'One button', 'One command', 'One document type' ), 'Storyboard the slice including empty and error states.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Eval on real drafts', 'Keep before/after pairs and a weekly quality pass.', 'You will save pairs and score clarity without new facts. Assistants drift. Eval is the leash. This lesson starts the set.', array( 'Before/after pairs', 'No-new-facts score', 'Weekly pass' ), 'Save five before/after pairs and a scoring note.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-legal-document-drafting' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A drafting aid, not a lawyer', 'Where AI may help and the review that must follow.', 'You will set the frame: AI for structure and first language, a qualified human for advice and filing. This is not legal advice. It is how to use a tool without fooling yourself. Write the frame on the wall.', array( 'Aid versus advice', 'Human review', 'Not legal advice' ), 'Write a three-line acceptable-use statement for your team.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Issue lists and outlines from a fact pattern', 'Extract issues and missing facts before prose.', 'You will paste a fact pattern you are allowed to use and ask for issues, missing facts, and an outline. You will not ask for “the answer.” Outlines are the safe high-value job.', array( 'Issues', 'Missing facts', 'Outline' ), 'Produce an issue list and missing-fact questions from a sample pattern.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Clause libraries versus generated clauses', 'Prefer your playbook; generate only against it.', 'You will attach approved clauses and ask the model to select and fill blanks, not invent risk allocation. Generated clauses without a playbook are how surprises land in a draft.', array( 'Playbook first', 'Fill blanks', 'No surprise risk' ), 'Map five clause types to your playbook names (even if the playbook is a folder).' ),
			lac_cl( 'Defined terms and consistency', 'A terms list the draft must obey.', 'You will generate a defined-terms table and check the draft against it. Models drift defined terms. The table is a QA artifact. This lesson is consistency.', array( 'Terms table', 'QA against the table', 'Drift' ), 'Build a terms table for a short draft and mark two drifts to fix.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Redlines and comparison', 'Summarize changes; humans still accept them.', 'You will ask for a change summary from two versions. You will not auto-accept. Summaries speed review. Acceptance is professional work.', array( 'Change summary', 'Human accept', 'No auto-accept' ), 'Summarize the differences between two short sample versions in bullets.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Confidentiality and vendors', 'What text may leave the building.', 'You will list data classes and vendor settings (training off, residency). If you cannot say, you do not paste. This is the operational close of the course.', array( 'Data classes', 'Vendor settings', 'When not to paste' ), 'Fill a one-page confidentiality map for drafting AI.' ),
		),
		'multimodal-ai-fundamentals' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Many inputs, one job', 'Text, image, audio, and video as tools for a user outcome.', 'You will pick the job first, then the modalities. Multimodal for its own sake is a demo. This lesson is the job-to-modality map.', array( 'Job first', 'Modality map', 'Demo versus product' ), 'Write one job and which modalities it actually needs.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Vision: what’s in the image, what’s not', 'Describe, extract, and refuse when the image is insufficient.', 'You will practice extraction (text in image, counts) and description. You will also refuse blurry or irrelevant images. Vision models guess. Your product should not.', array( 'Extract versus describe', 'Refuse insufficient images', 'Guessing' ), 'Write three vision tasks: two extract, one refuse.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Audio: transcript then task', 'Speech-to-text as a pipeline stage, not magic understanding.', 'You will separate transcribe from analyze. Diarization and timestamps are product choices. Errors in the transcript flow downhill. This lesson is the pipeline.', array( 'Transcribe then analyze', 'Timestamps', 'Error flow' ), 'Draw a three-stage audio pipeline with where a human can correct the transcript.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Combining modalities in one prompt', 'When to send image plus question, and when to sequence calls.', 'You will sequence versus joint prompt. Cost and latency differ. Sequencing is often clearer to debug. This is the architecture fork.', array( 'Joint versus sequence', 'Cost and latency', 'Debuggability' ), 'For one feature, choose joint or sequence and say why.' ),
			lac_cl( 'UX for multimodal', 'Upload states, privacy, and what you show while waiting.', 'You will design the wait, the preview, and the “we will not store this” line if true. Multimodal UX is mostly trust and time. This lesson is the storyboard.', array( 'Wait and preview', 'Privacy line', 'Trust' ), 'Storyboard upload → wait → result including a failure.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Eval that is not only text', 'Gold images, transcripts, and a small fixture set.', 'You will define fixtures for vision and audio. If you cannot eval, you cannot ship. This is the fundamentals close.', array( 'Gold fixtures', 'Vision and audio', 'Ship bar' ), 'List eight fixtures (inputs and expected traits) for your job.' ),
		),
		'ai-agents-plan-execute' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Plan versus execute as two seats', 'A planner that writes steps; an executor that only does the next step.', 'You will split roles so the planner cannot silently take extra tools. Separation is a reliability pattern. This lesson draws the two seats.', array( 'Planner', 'Executor', 'No silent extra tools' ), 'Write the planner output schema (a list of steps) and the executor’s allowed tools.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A plan you can show a user', 'Steps in human language with a stop budget.', 'You will require the plan to be readable. Hidden 40-step plans are how agents go off the rails. Budget the steps. Show the plan. That is the product.', array( 'Readable steps', 'Step budget', 'Show the plan' ), 'Write a seven-step max plan for one job in language a user could approve.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Execute one step, observe, replan if needed', 'Tight loops beat a giant plan that ignores reality.', 'You will observe tool output and only then continue. Replan on failure. This is the control loop. Fancy planners that never look at the world fail.', array( 'Observe', 'Replan', 'Tight loop' ), 'Specify what observation would cause a replan in your job.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Budgets: time, money, and tool calls', 'Hard caps in code.', 'You will set max calls and max spend. Prompts that say “be careful” are not caps. Caps are how plan-and-execute stays safe.', array( 'Max calls', 'Max spend', 'Caps in code' ), 'Write the numeric caps for a prototype of your job.' ),
			lac_cl( 'User approval of the plan', 'A confirm step before execute on anything external.', 'You will insert approve/edit/cancel. External actions wait. This is the difference between a copilot and a loose cannon.', array( 'Approve edit cancel', 'External waits', 'Copilot not cannon' ), 'Mock the approval screen for a plan with two external steps.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Traces for debugging plans', 'Store the plan, each observation, and the reason for replan.', 'You will log enough to replay. Without traces you cannot improve the planner. This lesson is the debug story.', array( 'Plan log', 'Observations', 'Replan reasons' ), 'List the trace fields you would store for one run.' ),
		),
		'rag-pipelines-from-scratch' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The pipeline on one page', 'Ingest, chunk, embed, store, retrieve, generate, cite.', 'You will draw the seven boxes and the data that moves between them. RAG from scratch means you can name every box. This is the map.', array( 'Seven boxes', 'Data between them', 'Cite' ), 'Draw the pipeline and label the data at each arrow.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Ingest and freshness', 'What you load, how you update, and deletes.', 'You will plan incremental updates and tombstones. Stale indexes are the silent RAG failure. Freshness is a pipeline feature.', array( 'Load', 'Update', 'Deletes' ), 'Write the freshness rule for one corpus (how often, who triggers).' ),
			lac_cl( 'Chunk, embed, index', 'The boring middle that decides quality.', 'You will choose chunking, embedding model, and index type conceptually. You will also store metadata for filters. This is the quality factory.', array( 'Chunking', 'Embeddings', 'Metadata filters' ), 'Specify chunking, metadata, and one filter you will support (e.g. product).' ),
			lac_cl( 'Retrieve: k, filters, and hybrid', 'Keyword plus vectors when names matter.', 'You will pick k, use metadata filters, and consider hybrid search for SKUs and names. Retrieval is a product. This lesson chooses the knobs.', array( 'k', 'Filters', 'Hybrid for names' ), 'Choose k and one filter for a sample question. Explain the hybrid case.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Generate with a strict context window', 'Pack chunks, instruct, cite, refuse.', 'You will pack retrieved chunks with ids and a refuse rule. Context packing is an engineering job. This is the generate stage done properly.', array( 'Packing', 'Ids', 'Refuse' ), 'Write the generate prompt template with placeholders for chunks.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Evaluate the pipeline, not the vibe', 'Retrieval hit rate and answer faithfulness.', 'You will measure whether the right chunk was in the top k and whether the answer stayed in context. Two metrics. Without them you are decorating a demo.', array( 'Hit rate', 'Faithfulness', 'Two metrics' ), 'Define 15 questions and how you will score hit rate and faithfulness.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-sales-outreach' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Research that is allowed and useful', 'Public professional facts, not creepy personal data.', 'You will list allowed sources and a research template: company trigger, role, likely pain. AI synthesizes; you do not scrape what you should not. This is the research bar.', array( 'Allowed sources', 'Trigger and pain', 'No creepy data' ), 'Fill the research template for one real (or sample) account using only allowed facts.' ),
			lac_cl( 'First lines that earn a reply', 'Specific observation, then a question — not a pitch dump.', 'You will generate first lines from the research template and kill anything that could be mail-merged. Specificity is the filter. This is the craft.', array( 'Observation then question', 'Kill mail-merge lines', 'Specificity' ), 'Write five first lines. Keep one a stranger would believe you researched.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Sequences with a human pause', 'AI drafts; you send; you stop when they say no.', 'You will design a short sequence with wait times and a hard stop. Automation that ignores replies is spam. The pause is the product ethic.', array( 'Short sequence', 'Wait times', 'Hard stop' ), 'Write a 3-step sequence with waits and the stop rule.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Personalization at scale without lying', 'Blanks from CRM fields you actually have.', 'You will map CRM fields to prompt blanks. Empty fields mean a shorter email, not invented flattery. Lying does not scale; it explodes.', array( 'CRM blanks', 'Empty means shorter', 'No invented flattery' ), 'Map five CRM fields to email blanks and write the empty-field behavior.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Objections and the next meeting', 'Draft replies; you still own the relationship.', 'You will generate objection outlines. You send in your voice. The ask is a meeting with an agenda, not “quick chat.” This lesson is the reply kit.', array( 'Objection outlines', 'Your voice', 'Agenda ask' ), 'Draft two objection replies with a meeting agenda in each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Metrics that are not vanity', 'Replies, meetings, and unsubscribe — not send volume.', 'You will pick two metrics and a weekly review. Volume is how spam teams live. This course ends on measurement so outreach stays a craft.', array( 'Replies and meetings', 'Unsubscribes', 'Weekly review' ), 'Write the two metrics and the weekly review questions.' ),
		),
		'evaluate-llm-output-quality' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Quality is a spec', 'Task success, not eloquence.', 'You will write a spec: input, expected traits, fail conditions. Eloquence without the job done is a fail. This is the mindset shift.', array( 'Expected traits', 'Fail conditions', 'Job done' ), 'Write a spec for one prompt or feature in one page.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Human labels that a team can agree on', 'Rubrics with examples, not 1–5 vibes.', 'You will build a rubric with an example of a 1 and a 5. Inter-rater disagreement is a spec bug. This lesson is the rubric.', array( 'Rubric', 'Examples of 1 and 5', 'Agreement' ), 'Create a three-criterion rubric with examples.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Offline eval sets', 'Gold inputs you rerun on every prompt change.', 'You will collect 20–100 cases. Slice by difficulty. Store them. This is the regression suite for language. No suite, no engineering.', array( 'Case set', 'Difficulty slices', 'Rerun always' ), 'Start a 20-case set with ids and expected traits.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Automatic checks you can trust a little', 'Schema, length, banned phrases, retrieval overlap.', 'You will add cheap checks before expensive human review. They catch breakage, not subtle quality. Know the limit. This is the harness.', array( 'Schema and length', 'Banned phrases', 'Limits of auto' ), 'List five automatic checks for your spec.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Online eval: sampling production', 'A weekly sample with the same rubric.', 'You will sample live outputs. Production is the only real distribution. This lesson is the sampling plan.', array( 'Sample size', 'Same rubric', 'Weekly' ), 'Write the sampling plan: how many, who labels, when.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Shipping with eval gates', 'A score below which you do not release a prompt.', 'You will set a gate. Below the gate, the prompt does not ship. This is how quality work changes the org, not just the slide deck.', array( 'Gate', 'No ship below', 'Org habit' ), 'Set a numeric or rubric gate and the person who can waive it (ideally nobody).' ),
		),
		'ai-for-healthcare-admin' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Admin jobs versus clinical jobs', 'Scheduling, forms, and explanations — not diagnosis.', 'You will draw a hard line. This course is administrative. Clinical decision support is out of scope. Write the line so nobody “just tries” a symptom checker. Safety starts with scope.', array( 'Admin only', 'No diagnosis', 'Written scope' ), 'Write a scope statement your team could post: in scope and out of scope.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Forms, benefits language, and call summaries', 'Draft from approved scripts; humans send.', 'You will use approved language libraries. AI drafts call summaries and form help. Staff still send. This is the first wedge.', array( 'Approved scripts', 'Drafts', 'Staff send' ), 'Pick one admin wedge and the approved source it must use.' ),
			lac_cl( 'PHI and vendors', 'Minimum necessary, BAAs, and what never goes to a consumer chatbot.', 'You will map data classes. Consumer ChatGPT is not a hospital system. If you do not have a BAA and a setting that matches policy, you do not paste. This is operational, not theoretical. Not legal advice.', array( 'Minimum necessary', 'Vendor posture', 'No consumer chat for PHI' ), 'Fill a one-page PHI handling map for one admin workflow.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Patient-facing explanations', 'Plain language from approved content, with a nurse/staff path.', 'You will simplify approved education, not invent medical advice. Always include how to reach a human. This is communication aid, still in admin/education lane as your policy allows.', array( 'Approved education only', 'Plain language', 'Human path' ), 'Rewrite one approved paragraph at a plainer reading level without adding advice.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Quality sampling in a regulated environment', 'Audit drafts the way you audit other patient comms.', 'You will sample, score against the script, and log. If you cannot audit it, you should not generate it. This lesson is the audit box.', array( 'Sample', 'Score to script', 'Log' ), 'Write a weekly audit of 10 drafts with a two-item scorecard.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A cautious rollout', 'One clinic, one workflow, one owner, counsel informed.', 'You will write a rollout that is small on purpose. Healthcare admin AI that scales on day one is a story, not a plan. This is the close.', array( 'One workflow', 'Owner', 'Counsel informed' ), 'Write the one-clinic plan with success metrics that are operational (time, error), not clinical.' ),
		),
		'fine-tuning-vs-prompting' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Two ways to change behavior', 'Prompts and examples now; weights later if you must.', 'You will compare latency of change, cost, and data needs. Most teams should exhaust prompting, RAG, and tools first. This lesson is the decision frame.', array( 'Speed of change', 'Data need', 'Exhaust simpler levers' ), 'Write the decision frame on one page for your team.' ),
			lac_cl( 'When prompting is enough', 'Format, tone, and tool use with evals.', 'You will list jobs that died from premature fine-tunes. If evals pass with a prompt, you do not fine-tune. This is the default path.', array( 'Format and tone', 'Evals pass', 'Default path' ), 'Take one job and argue in writing why prompting should be tried first.' ),
			lac_cl( 'When retrieval beats both', 'Facts that change should not live in weights.', 'You will put changing facts in a store. Fine-tuning facts is how you get stale models. RAG versus FT is a freshness question.', array( 'Changing facts', 'Freshness', 'Store not weights' ), 'List five facts in your product that must not be fine-tuned.' ),
			lac_cl( 'When fine-tuning earns its keep', 'Style at volume, structured output, or a domain dialect evals cannot get from prompts.', 'You will name the narrow cases and the data you would need. You will also name the eval that would prove it. No eval, no FT project.', array( 'Narrow cases', 'Data', 'Proof eval' ), 'Write a one-paragraph FT proposal including the eval or write “not yet.”' ),
			lac_cl( 'Costs of owning a fine-tune', 'Data pipelines, retraining, and model drift.', 'You will list the ops burden. FT is a product you operate. This lesson is the honest total cost.', array( 'Pipelines', 'Retrain cadence', 'Drift' ), 'Estimate the monthly ops work if you owned a fine-tune (hours, not dollars if unknown).' ),
			lac_cl( 'A choice record', 'Document what you tried: prompt, RAG, then FT last.', 'You will keep a record so the next team does not skip to FT. Process is the outcome of this course.', array( 'Tried prompt', 'Tried RAG', 'FT last' ), 'Fill a choice record for one real job.' ),
		),
		'ai-security-guardrails' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The threat model for an LLM feature', 'Prompt injection, data leak, and tool abuse.', 'You will write a threat model for one feature. If you cannot name the attacker goal, you cannot design a guardrail. This is lesson one on purpose.', array( 'Injection', 'Leak', 'Tool abuse' ), 'Write a one-page threat model for one feature.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Trust boundaries and untrusted text', 'User content is data, not instructions — delimit it.', 'You will wrap user content, put system rules outside it, and never execute tools from raw user text without checks. This is the core engineering move.', array( 'Delimit user text', 'Rules outside', 'Tools checked' ), 'Rewrite one prompt with a clear untrusted-data block.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Output filters and allowlists', 'Schema, banned destinations, and length.', 'You will validate JSON, allowlist URLs, and cap length. Filters in code beat “please be safe” in prose. This is the allowlist lesson.', array( 'Schema', 'URL allowlist', 'Caps' ), 'List the code-level filters for your feature.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Secrets, logs, and training', 'Keys out of prompts; logs redacted; training off if required.', 'You will hunt for secrets in traces. You will set vendor training flags to match policy. This is hygiene that prevents the boring breaches.', array( 'No keys in prompts', 'Redact logs', 'Training flags' ), 'Write the secret and logging rules in five bullets.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Abuse tests you run before launch', 'Jailbreaks, exfil tricks, and tool-spam.', 'You will keep a red-team list and run it. Guardrails you did not test are decorations. This lesson starts the list.', array( 'Jailbreaks', 'Exfil', 'Tool-spam' ), 'Write ten abuse cases and the expected system behavior.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Incident response for model features', 'Who to call when it says something awful or leaks.', 'You will write a one-page IR: disable switch, who reviews logs, user messaging. LLM features need a kill switch. This is the close.', array( 'Kill switch', 'Log review', 'User messaging' ), 'Write the IR page and name the kill switch.' ),
		),
		'build-ai-dashboards' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A dashboard answers questions', 'List the questions; each chart must earn its place.', 'You will write the questions first. AI can suggest charts; you delete vanity. This is product, not decoration.', array( 'Questions first', 'Delete vanity', 'Earn the place' ), 'Write five questions. Map each to one chart or none.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Metrics with definitions the model must use', 'A dictionary in the prompt and on the dashboard.', 'You will define metrics. AI narratives that ignore definitions are fiction. The dictionary is shared between people and the model.', array( 'Definitions', 'Shared dictionary', 'No fiction' ), 'Define three metrics with formula and grain.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Narrative tiles that stay honest', 'What changed, by how much, compared to what.', 'You will generate a narrative from the same numbers the charts show. If the tile cannot point to a chart, it does not ship. Honesty is a layout rule.', array( 'Point to a chart', 'Comparisons', 'No orphan tiles' ), 'Write one narrative tile that cites two numbers on the mock dashboard.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Anomaly explanations as hypotheses', '“Possible causes” not “the cause.”', 'You will prompt for hypotheses with data to check next. Causal language is banned unless you have a design. This keeps AI dashboards from becoming fake science.', array( 'Hypotheses', 'Next check', 'No fake cause' ), 'Write an anomaly blurb with two hypotheses and the next check for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Refresh, cache, and cost of asking the model', 'Do not call an LLM on every page load.', 'You will precompute narratives on a schedule. Dashboards that LLM on load are slow and expensive. Architecture is part of the course.', array( 'Schedule', 'No LLM on load', 'Cost' ), 'Pick a refresh cadence and what is precomputed versus live.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A dashboard you could hand to an operator', 'Filters, empty states, and a link to the warehouse query.', 'You will add empty states and a “how this is computed” link. Operators need trust. This is the ship bar for an AI-flavored dashboard.', array( 'Empty states', 'Compute link', 'Filters' ), 'List empty states and the compute link for your mock.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-real-estate-listings' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Facts from the MLS-style sheet only', 'Beds, baths, legal claims you can defend.', 'You will lock the spec. AI will invent granite and “quiet street.” You will not. Facts from the sheet. This is the integrity lesson.', array( 'Sheet only', 'No invented finishes', 'Defensible claims' ), 'Fill a spec for one listing including banned claims.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Listing structure that scans', 'Headline, facts, lifestyle, location, next step.', 'You will use a block template. Empty blocks stay empty. Lifestyle language must not contradict the spec (a busy road is not “serene” unless it is).', array( 'Blocks', 'Empty over fiction', 'No contradiction' ), 'Fill the template for one listing without new facts.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Photo captions and alt text', 'Describe what is in the photo, not a fantasy renovation.', 'You will caption from the image and spec. Alt text is accessibility and honesty. This lesson is captions as a product.', array( 'What’s in the photo', 'Alt text', 'No fantasy reno' ), 'Write captions and alt text for three photos using only visible facts.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Neighborhood copy without stereotypes', 'Public facts, schools as data not ranking theater, no coded language.', 'You will use public, checkable facts. You will avoid coded phrases. If you do not know, you omit. This is ethics and also good business.', array( 'Checkable facts', 'No coded language', 'Omit unknown' ), 'Write a neighborhood paragraph from a list of public facts you supply. No extra color.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Syndication lengths', 'Long listing, short portal, social teaser — same facts.', 'You will derive three lengths from one spec. Drift is how portals get wrong beds. One spec, three shapes.', array( 'Three lengths', 'One spec', 'No drift' ), 'Produce long, short, and teaser from one spec.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A compliance-minded QA pass', 'Fair housing awareness and a human publish.', 'You will scan for problematic phrasing and require a human publish. This is not legal advice. It is a professional habit. QA is the close.', array( 'Phrase scan', 'Human publish', 'Habit' ), 'Run a phrase scan on a generated listing and fix issues.' ),
		),
		'production-llm-deployment' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Production is a service, not a notebook', 'SLOs, owners, and a rollback for prompts.', 'You will write SLOs for latency and errors, name an owner, and make prompts rollbackable. Notebooks are not production. This is the bar.', array( 'SLOs', 'Owner', 'Prompt rollback' ), 'Write SLOs and the rollback plan for one LLM endpoint.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Packaging: API, queues, and timeouts', 'Sync versus async when tools are slow.', 'You will choose sync for short generations and queues for slow tools. Timeouts everywhere. This is the shape of the service.', array( 'Sync versus async', 'Timeouts', 'Queues' ), 'Draw the request path for one feature including timeouts.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Observability for generations', 'Request id, token counts, model, prompt version.', 'You will log enough to debug a bad answer without logging secrets. Token counts are cost. Prompt version is how you correlate evals. This is the telemetry spec.', array( 'Request id', 'Tokens and model', 'Prompt version' ), 'List the log fields for one call.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Capacity, caching, and degradation', 'What users see when the model is down.', 'You will cache safe repeats, shed load, and show a degraded path. Silent empty is worse than an honest error. This is resilience.', array( 'Cache', 'Shed load', 'Degraded copy' ), 'Write the down-state UX and one cache rule.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Canary a prompt the way you canary code', 'Small %, eval, then promote.', 'You will ship prompts like binaries. A canary plus eval beats a Friday full swap. This is the release process.', array( 'Small %', 'Eval', 'Promote' ), 'Write the canary steps for a prompt change.' ),
			lac_cl( 'On-call for LLM features', 'Runbook: spike in cost, spike in errors, bad output cluster.', 'You will write three runbook entries. Someone gets paged. This is the operational close of deployment.', array( 'Cost spike', 'Error spike', 'Bad output' ), 'Write three runbook entries with first checks.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-financial-forecasting' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Forecasts are assumptions plus math', 'AI can help the narrative, not invent the drivers.', 'You will list drivers you already believe. The model may help sensitivity language. It may not invent a growth rate. This is the integrity frame for finance.', array( 'Known drivers', 'No invented rates', 'Narrative help' ), 'List the drivers of one forecast you own and mark which a model may not change.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Clean series and definitions', 'Calendar, currency, and restatements in a dictionary.', 'You will document the series. AI that “analyzes” an undocumented export will hallucinate seasonality. The dictionary is the work.', array( 'Calendar', 'Currency', 'Restatements' ), 'Write a dictionary for one series.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Scenarios, not a single fake-precise number', 'Low, base, high with named assumptions.', 'You will force three scenarios. A single number from a chatbot is theater. Named assumptions are the product. This lesson is scenario discipline.', array( 'Three scenarios', 'Named assumptions', 'No fake precision' ), 'Write low/base/high with one assumption different in each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Explain a variance like a finance partner', 'Price, volume, mix — hypotheses to check in the ledger.', 'You will draft a variance narrative as hypotheses. Then you check. AI is the intern’s first memo. You are still the partner.', array( 'Price volume mix', 'Hypotheses', 'Check the ledger' ), 'Draft a variance memo with two hypotheses and the check for each.' ),
			lac_cl( 'What never to paste', 'Material nonpublic, full customer files, live account numbers.', 'You will write a paste policy. Consumer tools are not your FP&A system. This is risk control. Not legal advice.', array( 'Paste policy', 'MNPI awareness', 'Right tool' ), 'Write a five-bullet paste policy for forecasting work.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A monthly forecast ritual', 'Same pack, same questions, human sign-off.', 'You will lock the pack and the sign-off. Rituals beat a clever one-off chat. This is the operating close.', array( 'Locked pack', 'Questions', 'Sign-off' ), 'Write the monthly ritual with the sign-off name.' ),
		),
		'multi-agent-orchestration' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Why more than one agent', 'Specialists with contracts, not a crowd in a group chat.', 'You will only add an agent when there is a contract: input, output, tools. A crowd of agents without contracts is chaos. This is the design rule.', array( 'Contracts', 'Specialists', 'No crowd' ), 'Write two agent contracts for one job, or argue you only need one agent.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Orchestrator patterns', 'Supervisor, pipeline, and blackboards — pick one.', 'You will pick a pattern you can explain. Pipelines are easier to debug than free-form debates. This lesson chooses.', array( 'Supervisor', 'Pipeline', 'Debuggability' ), 'Choose a pattern for your job and draw it.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Messages as data, not novels', 'Schemas between agents.', 'You will define JSON (or equivalent) between agents. Prose passing is how context explodes. Schemas are orchestration.', array( 'Schemas', 'No novels', 'Context caps' ), 'Write the message schema from agent A to agent B.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Shared tools and locking', 'Who may write, and how you avoid double sends.', 'You will assign tool ownership. Two agents that can email is a bug. Locks and idempotency are part of orchestration.', array( 'Tool ownership', 'Idempotency', 'No double send' ), 'Assign each tool to one agent. Name the lock for the write tool.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Debugging a multi-agent run', 'A single trace id across all seats.', 'You will require one trace id. Without it you cannot debug. This is the ops lesson.', array( 'Trace id', 'Per-agent logs', 'Replay' ), 'List the trace fields across orchestrator and two agents.' ),
			lac_cl( 'When to go back to one agent', 'Complexity tax versus a better prompt.', 'You will write the rule for collapsing. Orchestration is expensive. This course ends on taste: fewer moving parts if quality is equal.', array( 'Complexity tax', 'Collapse rule', 'Taste' ), 'Write a collapse rule in three bullets.' ),
		),
		'ai-for-education-tutoring' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Tutor, not answer key', 'Hints and questions first; solutions last and optional.', 'You will set the pedagogy: Socratic, then example, then solution if the learner asks. A tutor that dumps answers trains nothing. This is the product principle.', array( 'Hints first', 'Solution optional', 'Pedagogy' ), 'Write the tutor policy in four rules.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A learner model you can see', 'Goal, last misconception, reading level.', 'You will store a small visible profile the learner can edit. Hidden models that “know” the student will get it wrong. Visible and small wins.', array( 'Visible profile', 'Misconception', 'Editable' ), 'Design the profile fields and an example learner.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Lesson loops', 'Explain, check, hint, next.', 'You will design a loop with a check question. No check, no tutoring. This is the session structure.', array( 'Explain', 'Check', 'Hint' ), 'Storyboard one loop for a single concept.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Worked examples and common mistakes', 'Show a mistake, then the repair.', 'You will generate a wrong solution and a repair. Learning from errors is powerful. The tutor should not only show perfect work.', array( 'Wrong then repair', 'Common mistakes', 'Not only perfect' ), 'Write one worked mistake and the repair for a topic you choose.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Safety in tutoring products', 'Minors, self-harm, and “do my exam.”', 'You will write refusals and escalation. If you serve minors, extra care and policy apply — follow your counsel. This lesson is a serious stop. Not legal advice.', array( 'Exam cheating refusal', 'Escalation', 'Policy' ), 'Write refusal copy for “write my exam answers” and an escalation path.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Measure learning, not chat length', 'Checks passed, time to hint, and teacher review.', 'You will pick learning metrics. Long chats can mean confusion. This is the eval close for tutoring.', array( 'Checks passed', 'Hint timing', 'Teacher review' ), 'Define two metrics and a sample a teacher would review weekly.' ),
		),
		'computer-vision-for-builders' => array(
			lac_cl( 'A vision feature is a contract', 'Input image, output fields, fail if not visible.', 'You will write the contract. “Look at this” is not a spec. Fields and fail states are. This is how builders ship vision.', array( 'Input', 'Output fields', 'Fail if not visible' ), 'Write a contract for one vision feature.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Classic CV versus multimodal models', 'When a detector is enough, when you need a VLM.', 'You will choose. Counting widgets might be a detector. Open-ended “what’s wrong” might be a VLM. Cost and latency follow the choice.', array( 'Detectors', 'VLMs', 'Cost latency' ), 'Choose a stack for your contract and say why the other is worse.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Data: angles, lighting, and the long tail', 'Collect the ugly images or the demo will lie.', 'You will plan a data diet: dark, glare, partial. Demos in perfect light are fiction. Builders collect the tail.', array( 'Ugly images', 'Long tail', 'Diet' ), 'List ten image conditions you must collect.' ),
			lac_cl( 'UX for cameras', 'Guides, confirmations, and privacy.', 'You will design the viewfinder overlay and the “we delete after X” line if true. Camera UX is the product. This is the storyboard.', array( 'Guides', 'Confirm', 'Privacy line' ), 'Storyboard capture → process → result with a reject state.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Eval on a held-out set', 'Precision/recall or field accuracy, plus a slice on the tail.', 'You will hold out images. Slice on lighting. If you cannot measure, you cannot ship. This is the bar.', array( 'Held-out', 'Tail slice', 'Bar' ), 'Write an eval plan with a tail slice.' ),
			lac_cl( 'On-device versus cloud', 'Latency, privacy, and model size as product choices.', 'You will pick based on the contract. Privacy-sensitive capture may need on-device. This is the deployment fork for builders.', array( 'On-device', 'Cloud', 'Privacy latency' ), 'Pick on-device or cloud for your feature and list two consequences.' ),
		),
		'ai-api-integration-masterclass' => array(
			lac_cl( 'An integration map', 'Auth, idempotency, retries, and the user-visible error.', 'You will map one third-party plus one model API. Integrations fail in the boring places. This is the masterclass frame.', array( 'Auth', 'Idempotency', 'User error' ), 'Draw the map for one integration including the user-visible error.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Idempotency keys and exactly-once-enough', 'Safe retries for creates.', 'You will design keys for “create draft” style calls. Double sends are the classic bug. This lesson is the pattern.', array( 'Keys', 'Creates', 'Safe retry' ), 'Specify the idempotency key for one create call.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Webhooks and out-of-order events', 'Verify signatures; make handlers idempotent.', 'You will verify, then upsert. Out-of-order is normal. This is production integration, not a tutorial happy path.', array( 'Signatures', 'Upsert', 'Out-of-order' ), 'Write the webhook handler rules in five bullets.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Wrapping a model API', 'Timeouts, fallbacks, and prompt version in the request.', 'You will treat the model as a flaky dependency. Fallback copy, not a hung spinner. Prompt version rides on the request for logs.', array( 'Timeouts', 'Fallback', 'Prompt version' ), 'Write the wrapper behavior on 429 and on 500.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Contract tests', 'Fixtures for third parties and for the model schema.', 'You will store fixtures. Integrations without contract tests break on Friday. This is the quality lesson.', array( 'Third-party fixtures', 'Schema fixtures', 'Friday' ), 'List six fixtures you would keep.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A runbook for the integration', 'Token expiry, quota, and vendor status page.', 'You will write first checks. Masterclass means you can operate it. This is the close.', array( 'Expiry', 'Quota', 'Status page' ), 'Write a runbook with three incidents.' ),
		),
		'cost-optimization-for-ai-products' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Where the money actually goes', 'Tokens, retries, tools, and idle features — not just the sticker price.', 'Most teams look at a model’s list price and miss the rest of the bill. You will map one feature into tokens in, tokens out, tool calls, retries, and cache misses. That map is how you cut cost without randomly “using a smaller model” and hoping quality survives. This is the cost anatomy of an AI product.', array( 'Token, request, and tool-call costs', 'Hidden spend from retries and long context', 'A cost map you can reuse on any feature' ), 'List every AI call in one feature. Label each as tokens, tools, or retries, and circle the two largest lines.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Measure before you cut', 'Unit cost, quality, and latency on the same dashboard.', 'Cuts without a baseline become arguments. You will define cost per successful task, a quality sample, and p95 latency. Then you will take a one-week baseline. Optimization that you cannot graph is just a vibe. This lesson installs the dashboard.', array( 'Cost per successful task', 'A quality sample you already trust', 'Latency beside cost' ), 'Write the three numbers for your feature and where you will read them next week.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Routing: cheap when you can, expensive when you must', 'A small model first, a large model on hard cases.', 'You will design a router: easy classification or extraction on a cheap model, escalate on low confidence or on a gold set miss. Routing is the highest-leverage cut that still feels good. You will also write the fallback so users never see a silent failure.', array( 'Easy versus hard cases', 'Confidence and gold-set triggers', 'Fallback copy when routing fails' ), 'Split 20 real examples into cheap-path and expensive-path. Note the rule you used.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Caching and context diet', 'Do not pay twice for the same prompt, and do not paste the novel.', 'You will cache identical system+user prompts where it is safe, and you will shrink context: summaries instead of full threads, only the retrieved chunks you need. Long context is a cost bug. This lesson is the diet.', array( 'What is safe to cache', 'Summaries versus full history', 'Retrieved chunks only' ), 'Take one prompt and cut 50% of the context without changing the task. Estimate the token savings.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Quality after the cut', 'A harness so “cheaper” does not mean “worse” in production.', 'You will rerun your eval set after each cut. If faithfulness or task success drops, you revert or you route those cases back to the expensive path. Cost work that skips eval is how products quietly rot. This is the quality gate.', array( 'Eval before and after', 'Revert rules', 'Keep an expensive path for hard cases' ), 'Run (or tabletop) an eval on the cheap path versus the current path. Keep a row for each failure.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A cost budget the team can live with', 'Caps, alerts, and an owner — like any other production spend.', 'You will set a monthly cap, an alert at 70%, and a person who can change routing. Cost is an operating concern. This lesson writes the budget so optimization survives the next feature launch.', array( 'Monthly cap', 'Alerts', 'An owner for routing' ), 'Write the budget page: cap, alert, owner, and the first cut you will try after the baseline week.' ),
		),
		'ai-compliance-ethics' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Principles you can operationalize', 'Fairness, transparency, and accountability as habits, not posters.', 'You will turn slogans into checks: who is affected, what they are told, who can appeal. Ethics that cannot be audited are branding. This lesson writes the checks.', array( 'Who is affected', 'What they are told', 'Appeal' ), 'Fill the three checks for one AI feature.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Law as a product constraint', 'Privacy, consumer, IP, and sector rules — at a high level, with counsel.', 'You will list the question types to take to counsel, not DIY legal answers. This is not legal advice. It is how a builder prepares a good meeting. Write the question list.', array( 'Question list', 'Counsel', 'Not DIY law' ), 'Write ten questions you would bring to counsel about one feature.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Transparency to users', 'When you disclose AI, and how you explain limits.', 'You will draft disclosure copy that is true. Fake “a human reviewed this” is worse than silence. This is user-facing ethics.', array( 'True disclosure', 'Limits', 'No fake human' ), 'Draft disclosure for one feature in two sentences that are true.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Bias, evaluation, and harm', 'Who the system fails, and a slice you will measure.', 'You will pick a slice (language, region, or another you can legally and ethically measure) and a harm. Then you measure. Unmeasured fairness is a wish.', array( 'A slice', 'A harm', 'Measure' ), 'Name a slice and a metric you will actually compute, or name why you cannot yet and what you will do instead.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Records and accountability', 'Who approved the prompt, the eval, and the vendor.', 'You will keep a lightweight file. Accountability is paperwork that a grown-up can find. This is the ethics of operations.', array( 'Approver', 'Eval record', 'Vendor' ), 'Create a one-page record template and fill it for one feature.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A review cadence', 'Quarterly look at incidents, evals, and complaints.', 'You will put ethics on a calendar. Cadence is how principles survive launches. This is the close.', array( 'Quarterly', 'Incidents', 'Complaints' ), 'Write a 45-minute quarterly agenda.' ),
		),
		'enterprise-ai-strategy' => array(
			lac_cl( 'Strategy is a portfolio, not a chatbot tour', 'Where AI creates advantage versus where it is a toy.', 'You will map processes by value and readiness. Tours without a portfolio become random licenses. This is the exec frame.', array( 'Value versus readiness', 'Portfolio', 'No random licenses' ), 'Plot five processes on value vs readiness. Pick one to fund.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Build, buy, and reshape the process', 'Do not sprinkle a model on a bad process.', 'You will decide whether to buy a copilot, build a wedge, or fix the process first. Strategy includes “not now.” This lesson is the choice.', array( 'Buy', 'Build', 'Fix process first' ), 'For one process, choose buy, build, or fix-first and write why.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Data, risk, and the operating model', 'Who owns prompts, evals, and vendors.', 'You will name a model of ownership. Unowned AI is shadow IT. Strategy without an operating model is a slide.', array( 'Prompt owner', 'Eval owner', 'Vendor owner' ), 'Fill an RACI for one funded process.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Value tracking that a CFO will accept', 'Time, error, and revenue — with a baseline.', 'You will pick one metric with a baseline. “Innovation” is not a metric. This is how strategy survives budget season.', array( 'Baseline', 'One metric', 'Budget season' ), 'Write the metric, baseline, and owner for the funded process.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Skills and change', 'Who needs prompting skill versus who needs process skill.', 'You will plan enablement by role. A strategy that ignores change management becomes shelfware. This is the people plan.', array( 'By role', 'Enablement', 'Shelfware' ), 'Write a 60-day enablement sketch for two roles.' ),
			lac_cl( 'A one-year roadmap', 'Two wedges, one platform bet, and explicit kills.', 'You will timebox. Kills are strategy. This is the artifact you could take to a leadership meeting.', array( 'Two wedges', 'One platform', 'Kills' ), 'Write a one-year roadmap with one explicit kill.' ),
		),
		'build-a-full-ai-saas-mvp' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The MVP job and the fake door you will not build', 'One user, one painful job, one paid moment.', 'You will write the job and the first paid action. Extra agents and dashboards wait. This is the MVP knife.', array( 'One job', 'Paid action', 'Knife' ), 'Write the job and the paid action in four sentences.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Thin vertical slice', 'Auth, one AI call, one saved result, one billable event.', 'You will list the slice. If a piece is not in the slice, it is not in the MVP. This is scope as a checklist.', array( 'Auth', 'One call', 'Billable event' ), 'Check the four boxes with the exact screens they imply.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Prompt, eval, and a feature flag', 'Ship the model path like a feature.', 'You will version the prompt, keep ten evals, and flag the feature. MVPs still need a gate. This is how you do not paint yourself into a corner.', array( 'Prompt version', 'Ten evals', 'Flag' ), 'Write the flag name and the ten eval ids you will have on day one.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Packaging and pricing for an AI product', 'Meter what you can explain: seats or successful tasks.', 'You will pick a unit a customer understands. Mystery token bills kill trust. This is packaging.', array( 'Explainable unit', 'Seats or tasks', 'Trust' ), 'Choose a unit and write the one-line pricing explanation.' ),
			lac_cl( 'The first ten users', 'A manual concierge around the slice.', 'You will onboarding-by-hand. Concierge is allowed in an MVP. Building a marketplace is not. This is go-to-market realism.', array( 'Concierge', 'Ten users', 'Not a marketplace' ), 'Write the concierge script for user number one.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Kill criteria and the next build', 'What would make you stop, and the second slice if you do not.', 'You will write kill criteria (no retention, no willingness to pay). Strategy for an MVP includes stopping. This is the close.', array( 'Kill criteria', 'Retention', 'Second slice' ), 'Write two kill criteria and the second slice if you pass.' ),
		),
		'ai-mastery-end-to-end-product' => array(
			lac_cl( 'The product thesis in one page', 'User, job, wedge, and why a model is required.', 'You will write a thesis. If a rules engine would do, you should not build an LLM product. Mastery starts with honesty. This is the page.', array( 'User and job', 'Wedge', 'Why a model' ), 'Write the one-page thesis. Include a sentence on why not rules.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Experience: the loop the user repeats', 'Input, wait, result, edit, save.', 'You will storyboard the loop until it is boringly clear. Mastery is a loop, not a pile of models. This is UX as architecture.', array( 'The loop', 'Edit and save', 'Boringly clear' ), 'Storyboard the loop including empty and error.' ),
			lac_cl( 'System: retrieve, generate, tools, eval', 'The four boxes behind the loop.', 'You will place retrieval, generation, tools, and eval. Missing eval is not mastery. This is the system diagram.', array( 'Four boxes', 'Eval required', 'Diagram' ), 'Draw the four boxes with data arrows.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Quality and cost as product specs', 'SLOs for task success, latency, and unit cost.', 'You will write three SLOs. Mastery is operating the tradeoff in public. This is the spec.', array( 'Task success', 'Latency', 'Unit cost' ), 'Write three SLOs with numbers you would defend.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Launch: canary, support, and the kill switch', 'A real launch, not a tweet.', 'You will plan canary users, a support path, and a kill switch. End-to-end includes the first week of production. This is launch.', array( 'Canary', 'Support', 'Kill switch' ), 'Write the launch checklist with owners.' ),
			lac_cl( 'Iterate: the backlog after v1', 'Eval failures, cost, and the next job in the thesis.', 'You will sort the backlog by eval failures first. Mastery is a cadence. This is the last lesson: how you keep going.', array( 'Eval failures first', 'Cost', 'Next job' ), 'Write a 30-day backlog of five items sorted by eval.' ),
		),
	);
}
